        <p>" LESTER, dear," said Mrs. (3 41dinfra, anxiously,to her husband, " I don't like t vo,igh of yours." "I'm sorry, replied Giddings, " 6AL it is the best have." MAT : "What's that for, rharlie?" Charlie: "That's a trap to catch th,_&gt; wrel iitt le birds that watch and whisper to mamma everything that I does."</p>
        <p>"You married me for my mo,,ey." she exclaimed, angrily. " Oh, well," he repli,d, soothingly, "don't blame me. I couldn't get it any other way, you know."</p>
        <p>Tun FARMER : "How much d'ye expect ter git ter that picter of my house that were painting ?" The Artist: " Oh, I don't know; probably £500." Farmer: "I'll sell the house fer that." " MOTTIER, what are twins ?" asked little Bobbie. I know," chimed in Dolly. "Twins is two babies just the same age; three babies is triplets; four is quadrupeds; and fire is oentipedes." MR. MEEKER: "But surely I ought to have some say as to whom my daughter marries?" Mrs. Meeker: "Not a word, sir! When she gets ready, she shall, if she likes, marry any old fool; just as her mother did."</p>
        <p>- "DARLING," said she, "do you love me as much as ever?" "Yes, dearie," said he, with his nose buried in his newspaper. That ought to have satisfied her, but she had to ask "Why ?" "Oh, I dunno. Habit, I suppose." "Now," said the professor in natural history. "take the case of a her. Why does it lay an egg?" "Because it can't lay a carpet," replied the bright boy of the class. A: "Why do you write to your fiancee with a typewriter?" B: "Out of precaution. She may have my writing examined by those fellows who can read the character from handwriting.'</p>
        <p>POWERFUL LIFTING MAGNETS..</p>
        <p>I The mighty triumphs of man's constructive . ability, says Popular Electricity, would be im- possiblewere he without means of lifting weights 1 —and heavy weights at that. The humble hydraulic jack and the pillar crane scraping the sky above the foundations of a tall building are . alike a necessity. Materials like bricks, stone, I timber, mortar, and concrete must all be lifted by rope, chain, •and hook, and much time is i wasted in fixing the tackle preliminary to the giving of the "right-away signal. With iron 1 and steel, however, things are different. They 1 are magortic, and can be made to adhere underl magnetic 'influence. Electricity clan supply that influence quickly and with reguldr strength; it s can as readily withdraw it—and all 1</p>
        <p>FATHER. : "Why did you let A nkiss you ?" Daughter: "Well, he was so nice, he asked me " Father: "But haven't I teld you pm must learn to lay ' No' ?" Daughter: "That's what I did say. He asked me if I'd be very angry if he kissed me." A BOY, carefully reared in courtesy toward his elders, was instructed, on going away to school, to telegraph home "Yes" if he found everything satis- factory. Ho did so, and the busy father having for- gotten the arrangements, and being therefore puzzled, telegraphed hack: "Yes, what ?" The answer came: " Yes, sir !" _</p>
        <p>_ CENsus-TAKvat: "What is your age. madam?" Mrs. Neighbour: "Did the woman next door give her age?" Census-Taker: "Certainly." Mrs. Neighbour: "Well, I'm two years younger than she is."</p>
        <p>WITH A SIMPLE CONTRIVANCE known as the electromagnet. In the industrial world this is taken advantage of. Large steel and iron works arc now fitted with "lifting magnets," which will raise bodies up ,to a given weight from any position. "Scrap iron or steel, for instance, can be lifted from a heap without need for hooks, chains, or tackle. It need not even be tied in bundles. Massive weights can be at once picked up, and while the magnet re- mains excited will be firmly held. When the current is switched off, with the weight on the ground, of course, the magnet releases its hold at once. Two magnets on different hooks can also lift up long plates of iron and steel with- out any tackle being required. In stock yards valuable time ,is saved by the employment of these handy devices, and they can also be ob- tained for small shops and works.</p>
        <p>LEA (despondently): "By George, my luck is tough; I just consulted an oculist about my eyes, and he has ordered me to stay in a dark room for two weeks." Perrins (photo. fiend): "hurrah, the very thing, old man. Take advantage of it. I've got 150 plates I'd like you to develop." FATIIER : "Do you think I ought to have my daughter's voice cultivated ?" Absent-minded Vial- tor: "I should think you ought to have something done for it." WHEN a well-known actor was asked at an ama- teur play which performer he liked best, he replied, "The nromptor, for I saw less and heard more of him than anyone else." WAITER : " Ronstbeeformuttonroastporkmashed- orboiledpotatoesbeefeteakpuddingsorpie ?" " ll'm ! Are not those things still in separate dishes ?" " Yessir." "Then why do you announce them as if they had already been made into hash ?" "WILL you love me when I'm old ?" she whispered. "We'll wait till you are old," he said, practically; "it's as much as I can attend to just now to love you while you're young." Miss LuLn FINNEGA:C ; "I will give yez me answet in a month, Pat." He: "That's right, me darlint, tek plinty av time to think it over. But tell me wan thing now—will it be yes or no ?" THE half - back seized the ball and made a suberb rush down the 'field. The crowd went wild. But when the cheers of applause had subsided it was apparent that the ball had not been "in play." "Oh, dear! what does he have to bring the ball back for?' asked one lady of another. "I'm sure I don't know,' was the reply, "unless he got an encore !" Sintotasr : "How's this, Jones? What do you mean by coming on dooty unshaved ti" Jones: "I'm a-growin' of my beard, sir." Sergeant: "Then you must get up earlier and do it, or —or--. Anyhow, you must not grow it while on duty !" " Do you think those two letters were written by the same person ?" I really couldn't say, answered the hand-writing expert. Oh, I see. You haven't studied them ?" "Yes. I have. But I haven't as yet been retained either by the prosecu- tion or the defence.' " No, madam," said the lazy tramp, "I'm sorry but it's ag'in my principles. I can't chop no wood. " ell." answered the farmer's wife, ''there will be some coal here this afternoon and------" " I'm sorry ag'in, but I can't carry no coal. But I'll tell you what E will do. I'll compromise. If you've got a gas stove, I'll turn on the gas fur ye." " Writ,, little boy, what's your name ?" " Shadrach Nebuchadnezzar Zoots.' " Who gave you that name ?" " I don't know ; but if I find Out when I gets older, they'll he sorry for it." MRS. SKINNER : "Oh, but.l wish I was a man!" Mr. Skinner: "Why, so, my dear?" Mrs. Skinner: I was just thinking to-day, if I was only a man, how happy I could make my wife by giving her a diamond necklace for a birthday present." PARENT (severely): "You sat up with young Spooner until a most indecorous hour last night, Matilda." Matilda; "But I love him! He is the light of my life!" Parent (testily): "But you know my rule! He is a light that must be put out at eleven o'clock."</p>
        <p>"Have</p>
        <p>Are we not</p>
        <p>A TIM years ago there was a man in Devonshire who had six or seven very corpulent daughters. When asked how many children he had his answer was generally something of this kind : " I have three boys and about 13cwt. of girls." -PERHAPS the largest hand on record is that of a policeman who went into a haberdasher's lately and asked for a pair of gloves. "What number ?" asked the salesman. 'Number 763!" said the policeman, a little taken aback by the question, and thinking. nc doubt, that the salesman wished- to identify him as a member of the 'force" with an intention to present him with the glove. "DoN'T you love the country when the trees begin to leaf, and all Nature takes on the freshness and full-fed 0,”,0ur nf imev earth. !1...</p>
