      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>POWER OF A WATER-JET.</head>
        <p>POWER OF A WATER-JET.</p>
        <p>The force of a jet of water may be truly enor- mous under certain oonditions. A contemporary asks its readers to imagine a perpendicular 'column of water more than A of a-mile high, 26in. in diameter at the top, and 24in. in diameter at 1 the bottom. Those remarkable conditions are complied with, as far as power goes, in the Mill Creek Plant, U.S.A., which operates under a Ihead of 1,960 ft. This column of water, which, if liberated, would be just about enough to make a small trout stream, gives a capacity of 5,200 h.-p., or enough power to run a good-sized ocean- going vessel. As the water strikellithe buckets lof the waterwheel, it has a pressure of 8501 b. to the square inch. It takes a powerful waterwheel to withstand the tremendous pressure. At Butte Creek, Cal., U.S.A., a single jet of water, 6in2 'in diameter, issues from the nozzle at the tre- mendous velocity of 20,000 ft. a minute. It im- pinges on the buckets of what is said to be the most powerful single waterwheel ever built, caus- ing the latter to travel at the rate of 94 'miles an hour, making 400 revolutions a minute. This 6-in stream has a capacity of 12,000 h.-p. The water for operating the plant is conveyed from Butte Creek through a ditch and discharged into a regular reservoir which is 1,500 ft. above the power house. Two steel pressure pipet lines, 30in. in diameter, conduct the water to the power house.</p>
      </div>
