      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Woolston Water Supply.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday when there were present: Messrs. J. Brooks (presiding), Messrs. G. Mather. W. Forshaw, J. Backhouse, W. Houghton, J. Ball, J. C. Sutton (clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), F. W. Herfoot (inspector) and Dr. Sephton (medical officer.)</p>
        <p>PROPOSED' NEW SCHOOI. AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Mr. SUTTON announced the intention of the county Council to build a new school at Penketh to accommo- date 400 scholars. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER : I suppose the Parish Councils inter- ested will have to take it up. A good deal has been said in another place and some parishes are going to kick against it. MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. _ _</p>
        <p>The—E.F.II—K--stated that he bad received from tlie Local Government Board copies of the proposed pro- visional order granting powers to the Manchester Port Sanitary authority to inspect foods passing along the canal. In face of what had appeared recently in the papers he the clerk) thought it very important that the authority should have those powers. The CHAIRMAN: It is a step in the right direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOOLSTONIVATiR SUPPLY:</head>
        <p>WOOLSTONIVATiR SUPPLY:</p>
        <p>At a parish meeting of Woolston on June 4, it was unanimously decided to agree with the offer of the War- rington Corporation to supply the parish with water, at a total cost of £1,164 on condition that the District Council should guarantee the necessary 't per cent., or the cost of the work. It was pointed out that the land and property owners in the parish had guaranteed the deficiency. Mr. J. C. Surros said the Corporation/were satisfied with the guarantees from the property owners, and therefore it was not necessary for tbe District Council to guarantee. It was decided to embody the report in the eptincil minute book and to adopt any course which might be found necessary in the carrying out of the work.</p>
        <p>THE OF GRIDS. Mr. BOUGHTON said that sometime on Saturday night or Sunday morning a large gully grid in Woolston was stolen and the road left in a very dangerous state. Mr. Newcombe informed him that grids were being stolen from all over the district. They were taken by the drivers of the rag-carts, and he..hopPd the publicity given to the matter would result In the stopping of the thefts.</p>
        <p>Mr. NEWCONIBE said that five grids had been stolen during the past fortnight. Ile bad reported the matter to the police both of Warrington and of the country.</p>
        <p>TIEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPTIION reported that during the month there had been 30 births and 2 deaths. The death rate was equal to 22•01 per 1,000 as against3l the average for the past three'months and 57.3 for the corresponding period of last year. It was thellrst time for twelve months that he had been able to report an excess of births over deaths. Eleven of the deaths occurred at the county asylum exclusive of which the death rate was equal to IPO2 per 1,000. Since the last meeting of the Connell there had been an outbreak of whooping-cough and measles at Winwick and he bad ordered the schools to be closed until June 14. The outbreak was now on the wane.</p>
        <p>SPITTLE BRIDGE. The Chnirinan, Mr. Houghton, Mr. Mather, and the Surveyor were appointed a coMmittee to meet the County Surveyor and County Bridget-nester at Spittle Bridge with regard to the improvement of the Bridge and of the approaches thereto.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>best of</head>
        <p>The best of</p>
        <p>diaper shops</p>
        <p>The _best friends of draper shops are wash- boilers, common soaps, and the womeu who scrub and rub and boil the clothes.</p>
        <p>A quicker way of hardly be discovered.</p>
        <p>wearing , out clothes could</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha soap was invented to make clothes cleaner and whiter and sweeter than with ordinary</p>
        <p>soaps.</p>
        <p>Not .only was the invention successful, but the only, right way, the best w:iy,,to use Fels-Naptha happened to be without boiling.</p>
        <p>So that the best way—" the Fels-Naptha way" happens to be the easiest and the cheapest way.</p>
        <p>FELS-NAPTHA, 39 Wilson Street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>friends</head>
        <p>friends</p>
        <p>rocks. But though there was some shouting at times from sangar to sangar, and a little promiscuous shooting, the enemy felt quite safe, and sang most cheerfully, and beat. tomtoms as if some tamasha were on. It was three o'clock in the morning when GwynTle and his man came back. Their hands were bleed- ing, an‘l both were utterly exhausted. But— "lt's all right," said Gwyane, cheerfully. "I think we can do it."</p>
        <p>_ He told his story to the Colonel and Blundell, and then turned in just as he was, entirely done up. But Blundell, who,was Deputy Assistant Adjutant- General and Deputy Assistnnt Quartermaster- General, and everything else, now that Hunt was laid out, stayed with the Colonel, and planned the ettack.</p>
        <p>In the niorning a mail came in from Karakabad and the man who brought it brought in telegrams from the ' last heliograph station urging liaste. Colonel Briggs swore. But Blundell opened a letter, and in it found a photograph. It took him some little while-to name it. "Madge Gretton, by Jove!" he said,half And, seeing Billy outside, he thrust it in his breast-pocket. ( To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
