      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The Spanish Wedding Tragedy.—To the young Kiag and Queen of Spain, the terrible tragedy of their wedding day must ever remain an ineffaceable memory which must also be shared by the Royal guests and others, who have happily returned unharmed from the blighted festivities. There is alas, nothing new in these Anarchist outrages, but to conceal a bomb, and toss it as a wedding bouquet to a young and innocent bride, suggests a refinement of cruelty and malice from which one would think the most evilly disposed would shrink. Yet not only was the attempt made, but it was done with a horrible earnestness to kill and maim, almost unprecedented in these crimes. The terrific nature of the explosion is shown by the long list of killed and wounded—the saddest feature of it all—and it seems almost miraculous how the King and Queen escaped uninjured. That they did so is a matter for heartfelt rejoicing, but irreparable evil has been wrought, and it is sad to think that the young Princess who so recently left our shores, should have had such an early and terrible warning of the perils that surround a throne. _</p>
        <p>Belief in Omens.—lt is a moot question whether Kings and Queens have surnames, who, for instance would know that King Edward is a 'Wotan yet on the same principle that Queen Victoria was a quelph, so that would be his Majesty's surname. It would there- fore be rather a stretch of imagination to see in the case of the King and Queen of Spain, and the similarity between Battenberg and Bourton,any reference to a very old,and common West Country superstitution. According to that, if a woman's surname after marriage begins with the same letter as her maiden surname, she will meet with all sorts of disasters. Other omens presented by the Spanish marriage will appeal more generally to the superstitions and notably the fact that the King of Spain Is named Alfonso the thirteenth. There is also the circumstance that he was married in May in defiance of our belief that May marriages are unlucky. The Two Lady William Cecil's.—One is the daughter of the late Earl of Lathom and is married to the second ' son of the late Marquis of Salisbury, and the other is the daughter of Lord Amherst of Hackney, and is the vidle of Lord William Cecil, uncle to Lord Exeter. It was the last named Lady William Cecil, who attended Princess Ena at her marriage with King Alfonso and was present with the young bride at the altar. Her husband is Comptroller of the Household of Princess Henry of Battenberg, and also accompanied the bridal party. Lord Amherst of Hackney has no son, although he has several daughters, and, as the eldest, Lady William Cecil is heiress under special remainder' to her father's peerage. She was married in 1885, and her son will come of age next year. June Weddings.—Society weddings are so numerous this month, that all the available days are fully taken up, in many cases the guests driving from one church to another, to attend wedding after 'wedding. Twp of the most important take place on the 14th, namely the marriag• of Lord Ninian Crichton-Stuart, brotter of the Marquis of Bute. and the Hon. Ismay Preston ; and that of the Marquis of Graham and Lady Mary Hamilton. , The first named will be solemnised in the private chapel of Gornianston Castle in Ireland, but the Graham wedding will take plsce in London from Devonshire House. The Duchess of Devenshire will give the subsequent reception which will rank as one of the great Society events of the season. The wedding of the Hon. Arthur Crichton, son of the Earl and Countess ofiErne, and Miss Katherine Trefusis, is I to take place on the 13th in definance of the popular thirteen superstition, and that of Viscount Howick and Lady Mabel Palmer' daughter of Lord and Lady Selborne is fixed for the 16th inst. Less Ceremony and Gayer Times.—lt would seem as , though the day of big crushes is over, for again this season the small and select party is the one preferred by htstesses. Moreover there is decidedly less osten- tation, menus being shorter, and decorations less lavish. Another welcome notion is that of issuing brief invita- tions for all kinds of parties, some of the gayest balls, and most successful dinner parties being arranged at two or three days notice, and then often by telegram, telephone,tlor a few words written on a visiting card. Coloured Linings to White Toilettes.—Apparently many of us have at length discovered the fact that pure white, or even unrelieved cream is not becoming to everyone, as the idea of pale pink, pale blue, mauve, heliotrope, and pale green slips, is to the forefront among leading dressmakers, Few girls, and fewer women I look their best in white, and the faint tinge of the colour that suits them best, shewing through semi- transparent materials and laces, proves most becoming. Pink is the favourite hue, but it should be well bourne L in mind that it must neveribe bright and decided, just a faint blush tint as seen through the gown, being the effect sought after. Collars and Sleeves.—A noticeable feature in all the newest gowns is the extremely high collar-band. This is either transparent or semi-transparent, but boned at the back and sides to a somewhat extravagant height. After the long run of collarless gowns' we were certain to proceed to the opposite extreme, but even so the high collar is very much more becoming, and certainly in better taste. Sleeves, too, are lengthening, many 'dressmakers building these to reach almost to the wrist I—which is yet another move in the right direction. ' Summer Wraps.—The daintiest little wraps of the Itriple cape variety are being made to wear with many of the muslin, gauze, or other airy-fairy frocks, each cape being edged with gathered satin ribbon, or chiffon frills. Usually thelabric chosen for the capes matches that of which the gown is built, the lining consisting of chiffon or Valenciennes lace. Simple as these little capes apn4ar, they need great skill in cutting and making up otherwise they look dowdy and amateurish, and loudly proclaim themselves to be homemade. Tinned Offal.—The gruesome revelations concerning American canned meats and other products, will con- firm the opinion of many housewives that a certain amount of risk attaches to everything that comes out of a tin. We have had disclosures or, thjs side concern- ing the manufacture of " table delicacies," and other preparations, but these have been of a mild character, compared with the revolting practices and methods of the American packers. Canned foods have an inutrrense sale owing to the fact that they save a good deal of time and trouble in preparation, and cooking, but it would be better to turn vegetarian, or live on bread and cheese.rather than resort to the tinned abominations palmed off on the public as wholesome food. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Examrner,‘ Saturday, June 9, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examrner,‘ Saturday, June 9, 1906.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>ABOUT DINNERS:</p>
        <p>Food affords small scope for novelty, but slight changes become apparent, writes A Society Butterfly" in M..A.P. Smart society grows more cosmopolitan, and it gradually adopts the customs of other nations. Many people now begin dinnev with fruit, and melon in slices appears as a prelude to the meal. Clear gravy soup is preferred to thick, and in hot weather this consomme will often be served cold. At dinners already given in London trout seems to be as much in favour as salmon, and if a second fish is given whitebait supplies the usual alternative. Brown bread and butter should accompany whitebait, and is often handed with other sorts of fish, such as soles and turbot. Potatoes are nowadays offered with fish at smart houses, and are almost always eaten with sal- mon. Hot ham appears as L., supplement to the ordinary saddle of lamb, and spring chickens hold their own in what is known as the " bird- course." Most people have one (mix&amp; hot and the other cold, but in hot weather two chaud- froids are provided. Salads and dressed vege- tables have become a marked feature, and some of the recipes hail from New York. Rich, hot sweets are never seen, but cream ice is served in many varieties. In some houses pate de foie is always eaten at the end of dinner.</p>
        <p>A SUMMER SHIRT BLOUSE,</p>
        <p>The shirt blouse is always a desirable feature in tho summer outfit, and for holiday time and travelling it is an invaluable possession. If made of print it should be very highly starched, and then it will be found to- resist dirt and dust admirably. 'Of course it will be seen that only the very plainest styles are suitable for this pur- pose, and no trimming save stitching is per- missible, as a linen collar and cuffs are the cor- rect finish for a mottling blouse of this descrip-</p>
        <p>tion. Our illustration depicts a/ shirt blouse of pale-blue ,print patterned with dark blue, and intended for wear with a navy-blue costume. It has a shoulder saddle back and Hont, and this is slightly pointed towards the centre. The full " skirts " are laid in wide tucks, stitched down for a little distance; and a box-pleat, buttoned with small blue enamel studs, finishes off the blouse in front. The linen collar and cuffs may be either quite plain or fancifully treated with openwork or embroidery. Poem FOR THOSE FIT TO FILL THEM. The comparatively new subject, known vari- ously as sociology or social survey, .offers a considerable field for the abilities of highly. educated women, says the Queen. The social economist of the present day does not by any means confine his studious labours to his own library or to statistics collected for him by others. He goes into the cities, the factories, the workshops, the slums, and gains thence his materials for the study of life. Often, too, he proceeds further, and tries new methods of deal- ing with his fellow - beings, in order to learn whether social diseases will yield to treatment, and, if so, to what particular kind of treatment. In all labours of such a far-reaching character as these he wants the help of others, and it is here that he not infrequently finds he can em- ploy women. The trouble is to find suitable women. A good scientific education—that is to say, an education which has included training in habits of exactitude and precision—is indispen- sable. But it is also necessary to have a certain capacity for dealing with all sorts of people, a power of initiating schemes and carry- ing them through. It becomes a question whether what is roughly described as the "power of organisation" might not be more developed in girls than it is at present. It would seem to be desirable in some cases that girls who are not wishing to enter the teaching pro- fession, but to undertake social work, might have some kind of intermediate experience swhich ittoyuld fisdi a fit them training foracist,ivein paersmoneaalsurree: afforded by the Charity Organisation Society and by the various women's Settlements; but. unfortunately, not every woman can afford, after a lengthy general education, to give her " services gratuitously. The posts for which the services of superior and energetic women are in demand are those of games mistresses, work- house matrons, and matrons of institutions of various kinds. As a rule, , however, the candi- dates must be young, must be highly educated, and must combine a good manner with a sound judgment. And, unfortunately, not many women can comply With all these requirements. , q'S WALKING COSTITISB. , A smart wa ng costume for a matron is shewn in the accompanying sketch. The skirt is of black taffetas, trimmed at the hem with a band of black guipure insertion between two stit:.•hed straps of taffetas. The upper part takes</p>
        <p>1k;</p>
        <p>_ ' , , . . . , - lA' .kti ~, 1%4' • , ' ' C'— . ~i.,, I:, .4, ¶a', , , : 'P'i;l: .• li, !' /• .. ~ ! • k,,, ,;,, , V 1., , I t. ' i I, 4' -' i , 1 , . 4/ * ;,,••, '. /44 .:,,•;64' k.,;„, ~,!, „.. Ts, '`• 4, , . ij ii4V\ / /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>JOE CLAYTON'S LAST CLOW,</head>
        <p>JOE CLAYTON'S LAST CLOW,</p>
        <p>(Copyrighl.) •</p>
        <p>By IV [TALL-I.AI E. TIRE BUCK, • Author of "Dorrie," "Sweetheart Gwen," "Miss Grace of All Souls'," &amp;a.</p>
        <p>The undulating black road leading upward to Doddy-cum-Dene is like a long snail with its back up, and the two chimneys of Dene Colliery stand against the sky like the snail's feelers erect. Until the very last Moment these two chimneys and the pit engine-house seem the only signs of man at Doddy-curn-Dene, for not until you abruptly turn to the left, to leave the chimneys on the right, do you see the quaint row of white- washed cottages, set well back, and with a liberal cobble pavement in front. They are known as Doddy'a Nook, and the revelation is so sudden that, though you are still on the public highway, you feel as if you are walking across somebody's private yard. This feeling is especially strong on Mondays, for there, in summer or winter, ten -or twelve housewives are in pink or white cotton bonnets, leaning over their washtubs at their doors as if the open-air is part of their cots. , One bright frosty Monday in December the housewives were at their tubs—big, unwieldy Betty Birkenshaw, as usual,, at a trough instead of a tub, and sitting on a three-legged 'stool like an immature table. Though the air was keen, the women worlied at only half-speed. They were glum. They talked very little. Now and then a pink or white cotton bonneted head would expectantly turn to the shrubbed cornet., round which all news entered Doddy's Nook. _</p>
        <p>- Between Betty Birkenshaw and the next woman was an unoccupied space in front of a locked up house with the kitchen shutters closed and the white bedroom blinds down, going black. It had the forbidding look of a home suddenly checked as a social growth by the police, and left to inevit- able decay. After a long spell of silent work big Betty huskily sighed from her stool, "Hey, hey Aw wonner how Joe Clayton stan's in his clogs—be now! Aw wonner ! " _</p>
        <p>"Trial mun be well on be this," said the woman beyond the unoccupied space. "Aye!" ,added the next one, with a pause in her washing to sigh and look up. The fourth woman, with a voice as hard as scaffold nails, loudly called: "It's a black-cap job, 'tha'll see!" "Then it'll be kickin' th' buckit i'stead kickin' his wife, this time! Poor Liz Clayton! Hey, but her had a scrit-scrat time wi' him. "Her hed," responded Betty. "Her hed so, poor scramblin, haif'-clemm'd craytur ! If theer ever wor a devil wi' hoofs it wor Joe t'wards her!"</p>
        <p>"Aw could carry his rope mysen--" "But, Lor bliss thee, he'll get off; his sort aline does! " called a woman from the other end—and as a man turned the corner. Every woman stood erect, some shouting: "Well, Jim ? " Jim put up both hands. "Lord in heavii! " exclaimed several, as they all shook the hissing suds off their isrms, and moved from their tubs. "Aw tow'd thee sot"</p>
        <p>--- "Tha donna mean it, Jim ? " "Oh, it's a done case. ' vri'out leavin' th' box. Joe's a dead man.' And all the women closed in upon Jim for details of the scene. "Aye, aye," said Betty, when they had heard all, "aw dream'd hard7i' coal last neight, an' it's allus a sure sign wime. Aw see 'd coal—an afire, too—as clear as aw see thee, Jim; an' aw seed it th' neight poor Liz gev up. Aw believed from th' first he did it!"</p>
        <p>_ "Though, God help him now," argued Jim, "he did more nor he wanted to, aw'll be bound." "Tha'rt a man, tha' sees," answered a woman. "He brok' Lizzie's heart, and then her back. God help him,' i'deed !—aye, wi' a kick! " "Hey, hey," sighed Ann Sillietoe. "Aw canna' believe as poor Liz hasna' bin doin' her washin' wi' us these three Mondays." "Her lies bin washin' wi' me, all this day," said Betty, with a huge rolling tear in .proportion to her size. "Hey, but he'll wish hard this day that it wor</p>
        <p>"Well, aw'd cut my firot off if it killed owt human--" _</p>
        <p>"The' mun be sure Joe Clayton," said Jim feelingly, "would cut his yed off if it'ud be owt of any use." i'He ought to hey' cut it off afore—not aifter I Hullo? What's Betty doin' ? " "Well,- aw nivver Actu'lly cleanin' Liz Clayton's windowsill I Well done, Betty ; well done! Aw'll go an' help—" "A.w 'wormer we've nivver thowt o' that afore!" "It'll look more like th' livin' among us nor th' dead," Betty remarked to the approaching helper. "Aw'll do th' like to th' steps," and getting a bucket, she Ivent on her knees.</p>
        <p>"Aw've ear'd tell," remarked Betty, puffing heavily as she washed the sill, "as drownin folk hey everythin' pictur'd' afore theer eyes as clear as in a glass, an' it struck me, Ann Sillietoe, as it may haply, be th' sane wi' hangin" folk, an' aw tho„lte—tty—!'th' 'idea o' cleanin' steps for th' eyes ov a hangin' man! Aw wor thinkin' o' Liz. Her kept everythin' so milk-white." "Oh, aw'm thinkin on her as well," moodily answered Betty, too absorbed to look away from the sill. "Killed folk, tha' knows, do queer things toward Kersmus,an' if Liz does come, her 'll see her cot front as her left it. Ann, lass, chalk th' Steps wi crosses—not wi' rings. ' Rings are rayther like ropes: Though now, as aw see th' cross-work, some on it shapes itsen like a lot o' galluses, an' aw dunna want dyin' Joe Clayton to think as were mockin 'him, an' a hangin' man's last sight Maybe goes on for ivver an' ivver. My verdic's underground i' th' six-foot seam be this." choensstesipenscuen!dbeurttir'rvoeofa my w dmaarn s a wheny t h , ."Sure enough, Betty, for it's the men,as brings to"-night! us all th' news." "Aw do wish day-shift wor o'er an my Ben well home. What dosta think ? Aw fair tremmle! He's weurin' Joe Clayton's last clogs! " "N—o! This Jde Clayton's? "Aw wish my Ben wor in his own; my sakes, aw do—now as we know verdic! Aw can see 'em, Ann Sillietoe, sec 'em, iyver sin' th' news coiled: an' that's what SW dunna like. Aw.ve a crimply feelin' o' something bad. 0 Lord! Aw do wish my Ben's feet wor out o' you clogs. Aw feel so queer that if he does come whossin all reight.aw'll slip a copper i' th' mission'ry box—an gee him th' foremost o' my mind! Tha' sees, when tha' gets a whole judge an' jewery thinkin' th' one way, it is murder !hen. Aw tow'd Ben that, afore he went this mornin-', hut he on'y--- What i' the name a' mercy wor you?" " \Vox- what, Betty ? " "Did tha' hear nowt? These windows rattled abooa me as if theer wor a pit-quake!" "'Twere th' sound` o' sixty-one," confirmed a woman, "when they brought thy fayther whosun —an' seven other faythers—feet first." "Hey, but its gettin' clarksome," remarked another woman. -</p>
        <p>"Ate fear," sighed Betty, "to go an' see if my man's meat is done, for a feelin' he mayna' want it." "If the asks me, aw think it's burnin'."</p>
        <p>.... -- "That's myt dream!" muttered cumbersome Betty, going to her door. "That's th' coal heroin'. Aw'd .rayther.th' thmit o' this day's meat bein' a bit late, Ben waitin' for it ; nor it early, waitin' for hint." She opened the oven door. A volume of steam rushed out. "An' tba'rt too like it blast for my likin' I" she said to the cloud, taking out a square pan. "Bless my life! "she exclaimed to the moat and potatoes. "tha'rt like to a pit firin'—man an' earth . so blacked together as tha canna tell which is which! Somar s wrong I—somewheer an' somehow!" she said, putting down the pan. "God in heav'n I " she cried, and hastily.' half opened at haphazard the big Bible on the dresser, to thrust her hand between the pages, as in a sacred refuge. She gazed upward with an anguished look of appeal for a few moments—as solemn as the moments of some- body's death. As if she had received a divine intimation to at once put away something related to the ills of destiny and fate. Betty anZdenly withdrew nor</p>
        <p>hand and carried the pan The back-kitchen. Two knives that were crossed were passionately jerkeil into a luckier position, and she looked at the clock. Her, eves seemed to make it strike one —two—three. "bay-shift's o'er," she muttered ; "he'll be movin' for whoam—if—all's well. Oh, dom them clogs, givin' me a twitch like this!" When Ben reached home perfect. with only a natural remark shout "floor Joe." Betty was like some captain provokingry wrong in his reckoning, yet extremely grateful to be pa a safe tack. Ben soon washed' off the black cake of dust turned into smears with failing snow, and was about to sit to a very plain tea when Betty coal: key said: "Ben, lad, ,aw'd shed yon clogs if SAI wor thee."</p>
        <p>That had been Ben's own thought down the pit • but the plain tea provoked him. He resisted, and because Betty repeated the hint, he twisted his , first piece of bread and butter into two, 'as if it: were one ,of her odd, fearsome, fidgety whims. co A long time after the first langour of hunger had been soothed a little he remarked : "We're goin' to get up a peytition." "A—what ?'</p>
        <p>"A peytition. For Joe---,for &lt;Toe Clayton!" "Toiask hun off ? "Not to push hiru on, at any rate:" "Avr wouldna' touch it: " and big hail tapped kile window. "Hark to you!" "Thy sort wonna' get th' chance, wornan." "Aifter judge an' jewery's verdic' ! No good'lt come on meddlin' th' law o' God an' thirteen men! "</p>
        <p>"Pooh! If a hailstone lint-Kilis it's a day o' judgment wi' thee." "Well, an' wonna wear clogs as hes mired a woman to death."</p>
        <p>"Th' clogs arena livin' it all, are they ? A bit o' wood, a bit o' leather, a• bit a iron, an' a bit o' brass—tut! Dunna think any o' thy notions i'to 'em. They mun answer to my foot—not my foot answer to them. If a bull kicked a cow, tha' d be afear'd o' havin' clogs out ov his hide or a spoon out ov his horn." "Aw would, if bull kicked her to death." . . . _ _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>"An' what's yon ftgle-fangle ? " he asked, indicating a clog-iron hung upon the brass rosette at the foot of the clock pendulum. " kW—picked—it—up—this mornin'." "Aye ? Well ? Is Kingdom-come going to come? What about it? "Well, Ben, as aw wor a bit scared sifter the news corn'd—aw hung it." "Hey, woman, woinan! Tha'd rob Calcraft an' hang thysen wi' thy garter if thrstockin'-foot stared thee too' much i' th' face of a mornin."</p>
        <p>"Aw've wonner'd if it's off—those clogs tha hes on. Just look, Ben, lad." "Get thy meat, good ooman; an' let me, som'ut like a good man, get mine." "Sly meat does me no god wi' you clog under thee. Aw'd eat happier if it wor burnt." "Or hangin', aw s'puse ? ' "Up i' some wood if aw did. Not i' my cot! Aw'd be afear'd n' th' iron once a year • bleedin' some o' Kitty Clayton's blood. Tim 'might please me, Ben, nn' shed 'en." Conie in • an' shalt th' snows off, or Betty here ineight tale thee for a gho-ast! We want thee to pack straight off to th' lawyer and get him to pen a peytition. All i' Dena pit are ready. v'ry man on us. Wilta ? " "If tlia thinks theer's the least chance—"</p>
        <p>Betty pointedly asked: "Aifter judge an' jewery agreein% Jim Holden? " "Tha knows nowt about it, Betty, woman. Rush thee off, Jim. We'll see thee made reight. Here's two shillin' now, if tha'rt short; an' Isaac will gi'e thee what he hes if tha'll look in theer." And off Jim Holden went, to the accompaniment of a long low rumble of hail thunder. Ben shortly saw a faint lightning gleam run down the bright clock pendulum. "Wor yon leightnin' ? " asked Betty. Ben decided not to give her that bit of nature to interpret against him, and did not speak. Betty, still sitting, turned her low chair on its rockers, and with heavy breathing bent to reach a pair of Wellington boots from a corner. She put them on the sanded hearthstone with a twist of the band which meant: "Here I when tha's done eatin', put on these." „ , .</p>
        <p>"It''s'a sudden sort o' Sunday, w9noli ?" "Aw wish it wor," and big Betty looked down at the dust-brown clogs on Ben's feet with the flushed tremour of some giant beholding for the first time in fairyland the mysterious presence of two dead moles looking as if they might take to their up- turned feet and walk the air instead of the earth. She had spoken. She had failed. Quite a child's tear came to one of her eyes, and she rose. "Aw'll go .to the village a bit." "It's snowin' hard!' • She put a big shawl over her head. "Then bring me come 'bacca." "All reight. But tha really dunna desarve Betty went to Adam the clogger. "Rasta apa r as'll fit our Ben?"</p>
        <p>"Aw'm inakin' these for him—see, to these owd ones ov his." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Aye, aye; but hasta a pair aw could tak' wboani wi' inc ?" _ _</p>
        <p>"Thes a pair on top shelf theer. An' tha can hey 'em cheeap—bran'-noo—it's just touch an' go they're not welkin' to th' gallus. Joe Clayton's feet wor goin' inside 'em, on'y—" "Rusin wi' thee! Aw wouldna' hey 'ern if they were -iron'd wi' gold an' nailed wi' diamonds! ilasta no other pair? " she asked, keeping her eyes below 'the level of the haunted top shelf. "Here, gi'e me Ben's owd ones! Tha knows th' measure be this." - - “Tak"em," muttered Adam.</p>
        <p>Betty grabbed them, hugged them, went home the back way, and peeped through a chink in the back kitchen door. She saw Ben at full stretch, putting on his Wellingtons as if he were a buttress for the mantelpiece. She would, not disturb him for all the world. It was a sacred operation. There was luck in it, Moreover, she had forotten his half-ounce of shag; and left him to complete his account witkh Fate.</p>
        <p>When she returned he had gone out. Her lower lip dropped like a pipe flange. It rose again stern and grim when she saw those two gruesome, murderous clogs all alone on her hearthstone. She snatched them up, but suddenly swerved— sickly. They fell. \ln terror and passion she opened the back door, cast her black shawl over the two horrible things, swirled them up as if in a black wind, and was hurrying them out when one, with a move- ment as if alive escaped her grip, fell from the )shawl down her shuddering form. With a passionate imitatien of what Joe Clayton's own kick must have been, she kicked out the clog to the white waste ground, hurled the one out of the shawl as if to the thickening flakes, and shut and bolted the doors as though the clogs, like frogs, could leap back. Early next afternoon everybody in Doddy's Nook but Betty Birkenshaw became suddenly aware that an ominous little black procession of men was descending the thickly mowed pit-bank head. "Theer s a hap, a, hap!" gasped a woman, straining her eyes under her two hands.</p>
        <p>"Sure' annual. Ah, thank God aw see my man."</p>
        <p>"An' theer's my Jim!" "An' our Isaac! Call Betty! Joe Hinde and Abram lienshaw's safe i' bed. It caia on'y be-- wait, wait, Betty; keep thee quiet; none on us can mak' it out who it is yet." "it's Ben !--"</p>
        <p>"Nay, nay, Betty, tha canna see that far—" "Aw tell thee it's my Ben—he's tricked me— he's done me dead—aw've just luk'd on th' shelf—he's murder on his feet—he's Joe Clayton's —last clogs on ; he's gone i' th' face o' love an' reason ! Q Ann Sillietoe an' Tibbie Bolt, corn' wi' a body and help to mak' way for hini.7-an' will some on ye go to Jeph Coke an' ask him to run his pony for th' doctor? Come, Ann, uw'm fair daft—nay, aw'll wait; here's Joss running. Is be livin', Jose?" . _</p>
        <p>"Livin' ? Aye! On'y som'ut's brocken." "What, Jossy lad? What ? " "Oh, tha'll see, Betty. 'TAN-ere a slip. But he's talkin' like a magpie." "Aye—witless! 0 J3en! why didsta, lttd ? Why didsta ? Aw lilun go an' meet him--" But three women laid strong. yet gentle, hands upon her. "Why, we can hear him talkin' o'er th' snow, Betty." "Oh, he'd talk from t'other side o' life, would Ben, as a mak'-believe," she answered, struggling towards the stretcher", on which his face alone was visible. "What is amiss wi' thee, Benjamin? What!"</p>
        <p>As the six miners lowered the snow-spangled stretcher Ben's black hand came out from under the coarse blanket to meet hers. In a pressure "ie tried to transmit some of his courage to her. "Oh, aw know thy /sand's all reight, lad; it's thy legs aw'm th ink in' on." • The coal dust twitched on his face. "It's no use pretendin' wi' thee, lass. Aw an hurt—an' badly—an' it's down theer--" The men broke in with : '‘l•Tow it's all reight, Ben. Tha's said enough " ; but he bluntly added: awmost be glad to hear, lass, that a fall o' block stone tuk Joe Clayton's darn'd clogs off me,,as clean as wi' a knif.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
