      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>JUNIt 9th 1905.</p>
        <p>Parliament resumed work BACK TO on Thursday, -inder the</p>
        <p>BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>not very terrifying shadow</p>
        <p>of threats from Mr. CHAM- BERLAIN. The raging and tearing campaigner has diversified the holidays by making short speeches to parties of his admirers at Ilighbury, where he lives. None of the speeches was remarkable, except as proof that the habit of prophecy grows upon him. He who has so conspicuously failed to foresee his own future is still confident in his pre- vision of the future of his country and ot the Empire Wise prophets are those who,like -MOTHER SHIPTON predict events that are a long way off. They may in the long run be confuted by events; but until that takes place they cannot be confuted otherwise. They can only, at the most, be disbelieved. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, more daring, tells us what will , happen a few months hence. A few mouths hence I—next spring, in fact—there will be a General Election, with the Liberals coming back in a timid I and ridiculous minority. Here Mr. CITAIUBEE- LAIN exposes his whole hand. He has never, to do him justice, wanted courage of that kind. The possible course of events on which his vaticinations are based can be followed quite easily. The Education Bill will be passed through the House 6f Commons by what will be described by the enemies of that measure as a tyrannical majority. It will then go to the Lords, and be so greatly mutilated that there will have to be an autumn Session to mend its hurts. Then there will be a tierce conflict between the two Houses,</p>
        <p>...</p>
        <p>I=IiMitigNIMIEMIM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>an appeal to the country, the clamour of a General Election, and a great Conservative victory. The country which so recently swept the Tories and the Protectionists away with the same broom will stand by the House of Lords as a bulwark of popular freedom, will restore the Tory reign of jobbery, wastefulness, and bribery of interests, and proclaim, of:course, its ravenous hunger for the dear loaf. It is a very pretty picture, entirely worthy of the distinguished artist by who in it was drawn.</p>
        <p>We shall not hear much about Chinese slavery at the General Election which will take place early next year, says Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, nor shall we hear much more about the little loaf. He for. got to say, apparently, that if these things are not heard of it will be because what is said about them will be lost in the heat of the combat with the Peers. The one question of the moment— a question to be decided once for all—will be whether the House of Lords is to remain as a permanent and effective barrier against Liberal legislation. We know what Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN would have said on this subject in times past, and how he would have gloried in leading the opposition to the Lords in just such a General Election as the one he predicts. His voice would have been heard above the trumpets. But we grow weary of comparing Mr. CHAMBERLAIN with his past—as weary as of listening to his predictions of the future. We may point out, however, that the Peers' first defiance of the House of Commons has not quite put heart into them. Even Lord RIDLEY, the young Chairman of the Tariff Reform League, and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S henchman, who led the House of Lords into the latest of its pit- falls, would now, it would appear, like to retrace his steps. Like Fear, in Collins's " Ode," he is startled at the sound himself has made. There are very large numbers of Liberals in this country. who would like to see Lord RIDLEY lead the House of Lords again. We now know that on the [Second occasion it would be at the instance of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, as We suspect it to have been on the first. "I am per- fectly certain that this bill will not pass" the discoverer of the British Empire remarks. . But supposing it does not, what force of opinion will the House of Lords have behind it? Mr. CHAMBERLAIN bases his belief in a great victory at the polls next spring on his now confirmed belief in the fickleness of public opinion. We, on the contrary, believe in the persistence and the triumph of strong popular convictions. Now, all the change of opinion which has recently taken place in regard to the Education Bill seems to us to have been in favour of that measure. During the recess there has been a great dying down of the war cries. Even the Archbishop of York has proclaimed himself in favour of a moderate solution. If the House of Lords fights, it will fight alone, or with the support only of a dis- heartened and confused faction.</p>
        <p>We note, with some slight THE DUST DEMON. surprise, that the Paving and Sewerage Committee of, the Borough of Warrington has been solemnly considering the dust nuisance and passing resolu- tions thereon. One would have thought that 018 was a matter with which they were onlj rernotell concerned, for the amount of macadamisei surface in the borough at sli liable to the motor-dust, storm is every small indeed. Perhaps the committee Wag actuated with sympathy for the authorities of rural districts, and desired to swell the volume of their complaint in the ears of the Local Govern- ment Board. There is nothing very illuminating or original about the findings of the Paving Com- mittee. They deplore the terrible nuisance end damage caused by heavy motor vehicles, and so do we most bitterly. The clumsy, iron-tyred monsters which shatter the road surface, break the sewers etc., and make their surroundings hideous with thunderous noise are a development of motor propulsion which nearly everybody abominates. They ought never to have been per- mitted on the high-road and we hope they will,ere long be banished. Our local committee then goes on to suggest the taxation of all motor vehicles and the allocation of the revenue towards the expense of road-watering. A tax on motors would scarcely be necessary:if the licence fees and fines were available for road improvement—but not watering! Water certainly lays the dust, but only for a very I short time, and it likewise creates a Considerable .1 nuisance itself, In making this suggestion the Committee shows itself behind the times. The efficacy of watering to obviate the dust nuisance is an exploded theory. A new class of traffic has come to stay, and our roads must be adapted to meet the requirements of the age. The methods of construction will have to be revised ere there can be any hope of laying the dust. Greater expense seems unavoidable and it is for the Local Govern- ment Board to suggest both the nature of the improvement and the ways and means of finding the money without burdening still further the ordinary ratepayer.</p>
        <p>Is Csristian Unity possible? CHRISTIAN UNITY. seems to us the question which naturally arises from the spectacle this week of churches of nearly all denominations joining in prayer for that fellow- ship in Christ which is everywhere deemed essential for the furtherance of God's Kingdom, but which so far hos been conspicuously absent from the ranks of all Christian peoples. Frankly, we do not think such a unity of thought, belief, or even effort ie possible. There could be nothing more regrettable from the Christian standpoint. It seems incomprehensible almost that the millions of devout Christians in the world should be unable to work together for the one common aim, the aim of Christianity itself. But the fact remains. The ,Christian bodies all go their different ,ways, each towards its own goal, in the belief that that gal is the only one worth striving to reach. There is something inexpressibly sad about all this misapplied energy, and to the ' unbeliever it is not merely sad but ludicrous. ' How often do we hear the sarcastic observation: "These Christians, how they love one another." The implied criticism was never more pertinent that it is to day. Education hss once again proved the rock on which nearly all denominations are driven further apart, and estimable as is the , prayer for unity, never did it seem 1n )re hopeless. uTphrePsi Deg andv.Jh the need is "for the is, John nge Ys roYvv°thoEbetter spirit—the spirit of mutual confidence and brotherly sympathy." The need is self-apparent and urgent. Est what is to foster "the better spirit ? " Prayer alone ? s , If so, then one is compelled to feel somewhat dis- ' pairing even of the divine efficocy of prayer. Presumably, such a prayer has ascended to the , heavens through all the ages, with no satisfactory 1 'results. There were never so many cleavages in - Christian ranks or so many points of disagreement. '.. I That is why one feels hopeless of Christian unity. But,with all the d'ssension and contention,there is one pleasing feature. Christianity continues to spread. The belief in a Divine Saviour is gradually compassing all the earth, and after all the coming of God's Kingdom is not so impossible or so far distant as the spectacle of dis-unity among ' Christians would suggest. The prayer for unity should he encouragel and made the inspiration of Christian belief,but even without it the:blindest man may see that the cause of Christ is aver moving onward and that the goal of all will some- day be reached. It would seem that only can this much-desired unity be brought about by atnnce supreme climax of effort conjoined with much Belt.. repression on the part of all Christian coin cnunite'_ Let us hope we are nearer such a ccnsumm Ithan seems probable. atlas</p>
        <p>(I</p>
