      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>The justification—if any THE COUNCIL AND were really needed —for PROPERTY Co u n cillor Pemberton's . PURCHASING. motion of last month in I regard to the put chase of property by the Corporation appeared in con- vincing form at the Council meeting last (Thurs- day) night. On the Finance Committee's minutes 1 was a resolution to apply to the Local Government Board for sanction to the borrowing of £34,000 for the phrchase of no less than `2,4 lots of property as scheduled on the agenda paper. Apparently ~ the Committee had seen the error of its ways and decided, in accordance with the new order, to publish particulars of its contemplated investments 1 , in property. Members felt pleased that the trans- actions at last appeared in the light of day, and were only concerned at the amount of money " vehich was required to complete the bargains. The sudden revelation therefore, that all these proper- ties had already been purchased,rn any of them years , ago,was like a bomb shell in their midst. Some few were acquainted with the strange fact beforehand, hut the majority appeared as ignorant as the man in the street that they were being asked to sanction the borrowing of money to pay for rurchases It which had been completed years ago without their knowledge or authority. It seems almost incredible that the credit of the Corporation could have been pledged to the extent of £34,000 without the majority of the •members knowing anything about it. In the light of this tremendous revelation the argument that secrecy is essential in the conduct of negotiations for the purchase of property falls flatly to the . ground. The secrecy has been carrisd too far, and We believe we are not far wrong when we state . that the sum total of money spent on property : Ipurchases without the knowledge of the Council as wl4nle approximates £50,000. It is a matter for Veryi great satisfaction that the old order has given P ace ta the new, and that in future when the Cor- poration is dealing in properties, the essential par- ticulars will be printed with the ordinary minutes O f Committes. Under the circumstances, as revealed on Thursday, the Council could do noth- ing less than insist on tne matter of the required loan being referred back for full discussion by the c,leneral Purposes Committee.</p>
        <p>LADIES' TAILORING. SUMMER COSTU Nt gs. DUST CLOAKS, a.cstvß STYLES, SkIkRT MATBRIA.Ls, BX?RT WORKMaNsHIP, MOIJEIRATH PRIMO.</p>
        <p>W. Flom KINON, LT J., BRI DG STRET, WARRINGTON;</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The appearance of tank Park at present must rejoice the Beautiful Warrington Society and the townspeople generally. It is really quite a bower of beauty, the rhododendron display being particularly fine. *</p>
        <p>Three hundred society weddings are fired for the presentrnonth. " Oh, the merry month of Jeune !" —"The Star."</p>
        <p>The land question is in THE STRUGGLE FOR more than one direction, in THE LAND. the immediate future, likely to become the question of the hour. Mr. Seebohm Rowntree, who gave us reeently a notable book on " Poverty : a Study of Town Life," is engaaed upon a work in the Companion theme. Next week the Select 'Committee on the Income Tax will meet, and "ill hear from the Agent-General of New ',,outh Wales how, under a scheme for which he was largely responsible, our cousins at 'lle Antipodes make their graduated Income Tax complementary to a land-tax on unimproved free- hold values. There the ownership of freehold land carries Income-Tax exemptions. We have a land question in this country which will become acute at how early a date we do not know. In Russia, by all appearances, no question is half so Vital as this. When the Government found they bad made the mistake of setting the Duma in uncompromising opposition, they returned to the expropriation of land as a subject which, under all the circumstances was perhaps not so utterly impossible after all. Something like 180,000,000 acres had been asked for; they talked of 10,0001000. But specially did they advise— Which is so much clap trap in Russia—education, technical and the rest, and reform of the present iramigration system. They have welded together the peasants' representatives and the Constitu- tional Democrats. That seems to have been the Main result of their conciliation. Ibtej / I There was such a great rush for the trams during the Whitsuntide holidays that even the Longford ser- vice paid. Evidently the /public are beginning to realize the possibilities of the neighbourhood as a health and pleasure resort. * * * Nothing i 4 heard nowadays as to the suggestion .Ide some time ago that betting news should be blacked" out of the newspapers at the Warrington Library. We fonder if the "observations," which Were ordered to be made, have been completed. * * * It is to be hoped that the glorious weather will .?untinue for to-day's (Saturday's) horse parade. We ar that the entries are highly satisfactory, and a fine play is anticipated. Even the local volunteers will represented on this oecasioa, as the transport 2ggons will figure in the procession. * * • "The King of Spain made himself popular, not only i 1.0 Paris, but n every French town he visited. A lady 'Old me (says "Lorna") that her sister, happening to Bce the youthful Sovereign as he was passing through tiogouleme, remarked aloud to her husband, Why Surely that must be the King of Spain." Alfonso turned round smiling, and said " Oui, c'est moi." • * * The policeman in the cotton mill is a long way from being an accomplished fact. Our Watch Committee has informed the Hine Department— and quite rightly too—that it considers police supervision to prevent time-cribbing quite out of the province of the police _Come. Most Watch Committees will, we should imagine, return a similar answer to the inquiry. The combined factory inspector and policeman is not a palatable proposition. * Variety, " the spice of life " must be very lacking , In the village of Crouton, for the " Widnes Weeklyl IVevvs " relates with unction the following thrilling story "On Wednesday evening considerable r. •t interest was aroused amongst the villagers by the lemporary breakdown of 3 motor in the vicinity of the Unicorn Hotel. The mishap, which was attributed to a lost screw or nut, was ultimately repaired and the 'Arty proceeded on its way.' *s* The second state of Sankey-street is little hotter than the first. Either the tarred macadam has been tiodly laid, or not allowed to set--or something. At "ny rate it has cut up with the traffic in an astonish- •Ll?g fashion. Just now the surface is one long succes- "ion of ruts,and the cyclist finds it as hard to traverse the shifting sands of the sea shore. There is good ground for complaint if our paving department cannot 110 better than this with the ratepayers money. "The- 'eTporary repair " of Sankey-street is the worst job °I its kind we have seen for a long time. * * The suggestion that the Madrid crime had been Oflg premeditated is borne out by a statement made me by a well known police official who said : This attempted assassination was arranged in Soho.' There are streets in this district of London seething with anarchy : there are one or two Soho 'streets I'lown as the haunt of the Italian Mafia, and others 6.,3115e the most disreputable of Barcelona ruffians.' :there is a tiny cafti not a mile from Wardour-street 'a the back room of which was, hatched the plot to aossinate the unhappy Czar, whose body was blown co pieces in St. Petersburg."—" Tatler." * , There is something very comical about the medical .I,l4sPection by the American Immigration Authorities. ;ye read that Mr. Franklyn Murphy, a well-known trish athlete who arrived at New York lately, has "can rejected by the medical examiners on the round "that decay has already begun in his tissue gto over-exertion, and within two years they car he will not he capable of self-support." This e(lt'ulds bad for Mr. Murphy, but he is probably less _cncerned about his tissue than the authorities. We 17,0nder whether Hackensmidt and Madrali would 'celvise be rejected on the ground of physical '44.atnese.</p>
        <p>\.,-' - '"'' •</p>
      </div>
