      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>• "</p>
        <p>MEI</p>
        <p>=MEI</p>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY PROCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>In Runcorn this year there were more people in the town than ever for Bank Holiday. Fortunately the weather was warm and sunny. The Upper Mersey Watermen and Dock Porters' Trade Union members paraded the town in strong force. In the afternoon the scholars demonstrated. The Church schools united to form one procession and the Nonconformists a second procession, and the streets were crowded to witness their progress through the town. With band of music and banners and bannerettes innumerable and the gaily- dressed children, the demonstation was one of great interest. There were about 6,000 in the processions. FATAL DIVE IN WIDNES BATHS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry at the Widnes Police Station, on Monday, touching the death of James 'Nolan, a married man, aged twenty, residing at Hibbert-street. Mrs. Nolan said she last saw her husband alive at ten o'clock on Saturday morning. He went to the baths with another man, and, after washing himself,dived into the bath at the six-foot end, and struck the bottom with his head. He was taken out and conveyed home in a cab. Dr.. Lindsay attended him up to the time of his death, which took place on Sun- day. He afterwards made a post-mortem examination, and found the spinal cord was lacerated, and this was the cause of death. A verdict of " Accidental death' was brought in by the jury. FRODSLIAM SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The annual sports of the Frodsham Cricket Club were held on the cricket ground on Monday. Remlts :- 100 Yards Flat Race (Open).—Heat 1 : 1, T. Light- foot, Walton; 2, J. J. Matthews, Runcorn. Heat 2 : 1, G. H. Beswick, Walton ; 2, J. Griffiths, Warrington. Heat 3: 1, A. V. Sutton, Frodsham; 2. J.Knight, Over- ton. Final : 1, Sutton; 2, Griffiths; 3, lieswick. Won 100 Yards Boys' Race.-1, Frodshara ; 2, J. Knight; 3, W. Hancock. One Mile Bicycle Race.-1, S. Evans, Chester ;2, J. Smallwood, Birkenhead ; 3, E. Gould, Bridge. Good I finish. Sack Race for Men.-1, W. Haddock; 2,3. Goodall. Two Mile Bicyle Race.-1, Isaac Hewitt, Stretton ; !2, J. Smallwood ;3, S. Evans, Chester, Good finish. 440 Yards Obstacle Race.—Heat 1: J. Harrison, Helsby; 2, Jas. Goodall,Frodsham. Heat 2 : 1, J. J. .4 Matthews, Runcorn. Final : 1, Harrison ; 2, Goodall Tug-of-War (six a side).—Runcorn Waterman belt Runcorn Volunteer Sergeants. Treacle Bun Contest. —l, .T. Fletcher ; 2, KinseF• Three-legged Race of 50 Yards.-1, E. Worrall and P. Roughsedge ; 2, A. Ethms and F. Widders. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>_ The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Wednesday morning. Dr. M`Dougall reported that dur- ing May there were lffi deaths and 63 births,as compared with 21 deaths and 54 births in May of last year.—lt was resolved to issue a precept upon the overseers for £165 for the next six months ending Michaelmas Day next for the repayment of loans on the cemetery.—On the proposal of the Finance Committee, the Local Authorities Officers Bill was approved,and that Sir John ; Brunner,naember for Northwich Division asked to support it—lt was reported that the provisional order of the Local Government Board for the alteration of the Manchester Port Order, 1901, so as to assign to the Manchester ; Ports Sanitary Authority the iyawers of an urban dis- trict council under certain sections of the Public Health 1 Acts, 1875, and of the Public Health Acts Amendment Act, 1890, relating to unsound food, would be shortly ; submitted to Parliament. —The Law-clerk said the ; Council had supported the order, and in view of the recent revelations from America it was very desirable that more facilities for inspection of food should be 1 given.—Mr. Posnett, one of the directors of the Widnes and Runcorn Transporter Bridge, said the traffic on ; Whit Monday had exceeded all previous records. There were 15,000 passengers, 1,106 bicycles, and 197 vehicles. The population of Runcorn was practically doubled for I that day.</p>
      </div>
