ates and frieads. An ideal attracted great attention. Mr. S. Brown was starter, girl should be modest, homely, honest, and kind to Messrs. W. Holland and A. Byron secretaries, Messrs: everybody. She should also be very helpful to old ;J. Hatton, T. Thompson, C. Thistlethwaite, and the people, her parents, and younger children, who cannot et Iseer Vu tvies o ere judges, and the class committee carried out f stewards. Miss Bardsley presented the help themselves (or any others). She should try to be I prizes, everything passim off successfully. . The Sunday clean, teachers, headed passing Mr. and Mrs Weatherilaty, clean, neat and tidy always ; and should not associate 1 with rude, vulgar, or low girls, but should look upon and others superintended the arrangements for feeding them as far behind her in manners, cleanliness and 1 all the children. The schools connected with St. education. But an ideal girl should not look down Thomas's Church held their field day in Knowsley-road, upon poor, helpless or maimed men and women, or when races, games, and other amusements were enjoyed, I even children upon whom ' Dame Fortune' has turned 1 ROMAN CATHOLIC PROCESSION. her back. An ideal girl is not a vain, proud or silly RESOLUTION ON THE EDUCATION BILL. person, but a comely lady. Even if the girl is poor The Whit-Monday procession of St. Helens Roman she should not give up all hope of ever being an ideal Catholics was carried out on the most imposing lines. girl, because she must remember it takes far better The demonstration was almost exclusively confined to things to make an ideal girl than this world's riches, members of the guilds and societies of the town, but of Honesty, homeliness, manners and lowliness could net these over 20,000 turned out, accompanied by eighteen be bought with Rockfeller's millions. Ve: y seldom brass bands. The feature of the procession was that it an ideal girl is very rich, because riches often tempt was intended in every way to emphasise the objection and poison any person's mind who possess riches to of the Roman Catholics to the new Education Bill. any extent. Ideal girls are few and far between. Any Every one of the 20,000 wore a coloured badge' i " Catholic Schools for Catholic Children." In addition mother whose daughter is an ideal girl should thin to to the magnificent banners of the various schools,mottoes herself vely lucky because ideal girls are very scarce.- 1 were plentifully displayed. One banner from the Thatto Heath schools was of a somewhat lurid des- cription, depicting the carrying off of the Catholic schools by a most undesirable personage, who was warned in the phrase ," Hands off." "No surrender," "Catholic money, Catholic control, fight for our little ones, fight for our God," and" Our s:thools we build, our rates we pay, our schools we keep," were some of the mottoes. The schools assembled in the neighbour- hood of the Town Hall, and passed in front of the steps where the Mayor and Mayoress (Mr. and Mrs. H. Martin) and Mr. T. Glover, the borough member, were standing. A beautiful shower bouquet of red roses was presented to the Mayoress by Miss Katie Walsh, who was presented by the Revs. Father Hearne and Father Proctor.—The band of the Holy Cross, Liverpool, led I the procession in front of Holy Cross guilds, the other churches represented being Lowe House, Sacred Heart, Blackbrook, Derbyshire Hill, Peasley Cross, Sutton, St. Austin's (Thatto Heath), and St. Thomas of Canterbury. After proceeding round the town in the bright sun- shine, and through large crowds, the various sections of the procession proceeded to their churches, where ' Benediction service was held.—The representatives of i processionoostioe o,f dt the e present the various churches met and unanimously passed the solemnly following resolution:—"We, the Catholics of St. Helens, assembledprotest against Education Bill, which aims to destroy the religious character of our schools. Secondly, we further condemn the Bill as wholly unjust, and in the name or libeity, protest against the attempt to impose upon the whole community a system of education based on the principles that find favour with but one section ; and we reaffirm our claims made before the iotroduction of the Bill, and still declare our minimum to be Catholic teaching during school hours in Catholic schools by. Catholic teachers. We further pledge ourselves to resist to the uttermost the unjust and inequitable treatment to which the Bill threatens to subject us."</p>
