      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>AN OLD FINE.</p>
        <p>- -- - William Lee was summoned at Newton on Thursday last week to show cause why he should not be com- mitted to prison, he having failed to pay part of a fine and costs upon him in this Court in November, 1904 The fine and costs amounted to £1 Ss. 6d. ;he was allowed time for payment, and had paid all but 55., which the police could not recover.—Defendant, who behaved very impudently in Court, was allowed another month or then to go to prison, and left the Court say- ing he would not mind haVing a month in gaol if they would give him back the money he had paid.</p>
        <p>FATAL FALL DOWNSTAIRS. Mr. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest last week at Earlestown Town Hall, on the body of Mrs. Ann Coyne aged 64 years, who was killed by a fall do wustairs at her house, 73, Legh-street. Her husband James Coyne, a labourer in the employ of the District Council, deposed that he went to the INlarket on Saturday night about 9 o'clock, leaving his wife at home, and when he, went in again about half past ten she was lying at the foot of the stairs, dying. She died very soon after he found her. P.C. Lennie spoke to finding a wound at the back of her head, such as might be caused by a fall downstairs, and the jury found a verdict of "Accidental death." WESLEYAN METHODIST dIIANGES.</p>
        <p>There will by a considerable number of changes in the Liverpool district at the ensuing Conference. Some of these affect ministers well known in this district. There will be at least three changes in the St. Helens circuit. The Rev. David Jones. of Colne, a former superintendent in the circuit, who was to become a supernumerary at Conference, has consented to take the superintendency for one year—the position is unexpect- edly vacant by the lamented death of the Rev. Thomas Hollis—after which the Rev. William Collett, now of Seacombe, will come to the circuit. The Rev. F. R. Smith, of Earlestown, author of "Clogshop Chronicles," will return to Eccles circuit after three years' absence, and will be followed by the Rev. Simon C. Hall, of Huddersfield ; and the Rev. Harold W. Perkins, 8.A., will leave for Dorking (Surrey), and be succeeded by the Rev. Robert S. Armsby, 8.A., of Sheffield (Park). The Rev. R. Moffatt Ganirey, of Marsh-lane, Bootle, removes to Birkenhead, in place of the Rev. R. W. Watson, who goes to Glasgow St. John's The Rev. Philip R. Nsmore, who six years ago left Earlestown for Morecambe, is returning from Burton-on-Trent to Morecambe, and the Rev. C. E. Dove goes to York.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>REV. SDION C. HALL.</head>
        <p>REV. SDION C. HALL.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers may be interested to know some- thing of the career of the new minister of the Wesleyan Church who will come into residence at Earlestown next September. It may be that the new manse now</p>
        <p>in course of erection in Crow-lane, East, Newton, will be ready by that time. The work is in a forward state and a residence of noble proportions will be the result Despite the unfortunate breakdown in the Rev. F. R. Smith's health soon after his settlement here he has worked very hard and won the appreciation of many outside the Wesleyan community. It requires a strong man to follow himl and we understand that it is felt that a happy selection has been made in inviting the Rev. Simon C. Hall, who is at present a colleague of the Rev. C. E. Dove, a previous minister at Earlestown. The Rev. Simon C. Hall comes of a long line of Methodist missionary workers, In 1887, after a shortened course at Didsbury College, he went out to Anamabu, on the Gold Coast, to take up pioneer work. Only after much advice and struggle did he obey the doctors' orders and return to England, in December, 1890. After a period of rest and recuperation, in Sep- tember, 1891, he received an invitation to Bolton, which he declired in consequence of his health, not being fully established. de accepted, instead, Clitheroe, where pure air and a delightful scenery soon wrought wonders in his constitution. After three very happy years here he removed to Rawtenstall, staying the usual time with much acceptance. Then came Bacup, Newport (Mon.), and Huddersfield (Gledholt). Before going to Newport, Mr. Hall served seven years as foreign missionary secre- tary of the Bolton district, and the missionary exhibi- tion which the Manchester and Bolton districts pro- moted in May, 1900, and which proved so suacessful, was mainly due to his organizing ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
