      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>TRAINING COLLEGE CONCERT. The Development of the English</head>
        <p>TRAINING COLLEGE CONCERT. The Development of the English</p>
        <p>Anthem.</p>
        <p>The annual concert in connection with the Warrington Training College—one of the most pleasing of local musical events—took place on Wednesday evening. It has always been the custom for the concert to form part of the examination in music but on this occasion the examiner (Dr. Arthur Somervell) was unable to attend owing to indisposition. It was not until Wednesday that this became known and therefore the interesting programme which had been arranged was submitted as usual by the students assisted by a number of local vocalists and instrumentalists. At the invitation of the Principal (Canon Morley Stevenson) there was a large gathering of well known towns- people and the music was thoroughly appreciated. A new departure was made this year in the selection of items illustrative of the development of the English anthem. The experiment proved highly successful and enabled the audience to follow the various changes in this class of music from its earliest times. Before the concert began, the Principal briefly expressed regret at the absence of Dr. Somervell whose interesting remarks they always looked forward to with pleasure. Continu- ing, he said the anthem as they knew it in COMEIOR use now, was the product of the Church of England. He did ot think anyone would dispute that. All the cora- pos rs of anthems from the time of Queen Elizabeth to comparatively recent times had been the organists and great musicians of the cathedrals of England. Therefore, although, of course, there were many modern composers who were not members of the Church of England, he thought they might fairly claim that the anthem had i been the product n the past of their Church. As to the origin of the anthem, after the services were changed from Latin into English there arose a want of something to be sung in the services in English words. So we had, dating from the reign of Queen Elizabeth, what we now called the anthem. Of course the anthems I , of that period were very different from the anthems of 1 the present day. The first anthems were extremely simple and more of the character of what we should call a hymn tune. As examples of the early anthem, the students then sang " All people that on earth do dwell " (Tanis) and 1 "Bow Thine ear, 0 Lord " (Byrde)—compositions, as 1 Canon Stevenson remarked, of a rather severe type. The latter, it was pointed out, was considered a most beautiful example of perfectly strict music—a feature which distinguished the early anthems. They had not! the free play of the modern compositions and never 1 transgressed the rules of harmony. The next items submitted showed the change which I took place in the reign of Charles 11, who, Canoil 1 Stevenson remarked, was accustomed to a more florid I style of music and sent English musicians to France to cultivate it. When they returned they introduced the lighter style of music into the English anthem and for the first time there were solos, duets, trios, and recita- tives, instead of the continuous chorus. As specimens 1 of this ,period the students presented two very beautiful works ' I will arise (Cre)]ghtou) and " Hear my prayer, 0 God" (Kent), the latter being particularly effective. Passing on to the eighteenth century, Canon STEVEN- SON explained that the itntherh developed on the same lines, becoming more florid and more expressive of the words, and altogether much freer from the rules which bound the old composers. The works selected for this period were "0 where shall wisdom be found" I —a very striking anthem by Boyce—and a beautifully I expressivellmelody : " Come Holy Ghost " (Alt wood). I Before the modern period was entered upon, Canon i STEVENSON explained that it was one of which we had no reason to be ashamed. Great names and great com- posers were found in this stage of the English anthem i and the difficulty was not so much to know where to I find anthems as to choose from the very large number 1 of beautiful anthems which we possessed. Three 1 choice modern compositions brought the programme to a close. They were "Blessed be the God and Father " (Samuel S. Wesley)—in which the meaning of the words is brought out in exquisite melody—" The Wilderness " (Goss)—a fine descriptive work—and "The Sun shall be no more Thy light by Day " (Rev. H. 11. Woodward M.A.) Canon Stevenson remarked that the last piece was written by a living composer and ease a very fair specimen of the devotional anthem of the present day. , It is due to the performers to say that all the anthems ' were given with great taste and expression. While full justice was done to the choruses, the solo parts were admirably interpreted. The principal vocalists were : Miss Bertha Feber, Miss Ella Wilson, Miss Hannah Fawcett, Miss Amelia Wyer, Miss Agnes Freeman, Mr. J. Parkinson, and Mr. J. Bidchilph Poole. A small orchestra was in attendance, composed as follows : Ist violin, Mr. R. H. Pearson ; "nd violin, Mr. E. H. Ciare ; viola, Mr. G. Johnson ; violoncello, Mr. A. E. Wright ; bass, Mr. S. Sudworth ; pianoforte, Miss Hackett ; harmonium, Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bao. Mr. W. S. Woods wielded the baton with his custom- ry ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Corning Events. A brass band. contest, at which some of the leading bands in the country will compete, will be held at Warrington on the 23rd inst., under the auspices of Messrs. Cro.sfield's Band. The annual re-union service in connection with the Blue Coat Brotherly Society will be held on the 17th inst. Public meetings in connection with the General Synod of the Reformed Episcopal Church will be held at the Emmanuel Church on June 11th and in the Church Institute on June 12th. Addresses will be delivered by Commander Robinson, Revs. T. W Bowman, I'. Heath, H. S. Gregory, and S. Naish. BABY CARRIAGES. Latest patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Cricket --Warrington v. Preston. Played at Preston on Thursday. The visitors had only a weak team, and they had also the misfortune to lose the services of Thewlis who was compelled to retire after bowling three overs.. Score : Preston 193 for 5 (inniags declared) Warrington 105 for G. ' STILL LEADING, the "HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES and " PEVERIL," FIRE GRATES Sole Agent.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-at., Warrington.—Advt. . Music in the Park. The Borough Brass Bind will play the following , programme in th 6 park on Sunday : —Hymn, " Egremont," J. A. Gteenwood; March "Australasian " W. Rimmer ; overture, "John 0. Gaunt, " W. 'Zimmer; selections," Spohr," H. Round; selec- tions, "Satanella," M. W. Balfe ; fantasia " The Foresters Feast," A. Calvert; "God save the King " ; conductor, Mr. T. Griffin. - LAWN TENNIS, CROQUET, and other outdoor sports.—J. S. GARLAND, 51,Bridge-st.—Advt. Death of an Active Service Volunteer. One of Warrington's active service volunteers ,passed away on Monday in th,e person of Mr. William Heesom, an ex-private of the Ist V. B. South Lanca- shire Regiment. The deceased had only been ill a very short time, the cause of death being penumonia. He joined the Volunteer battalion in February 189 i and in 1000 he went out to South Africa with the first contingent under Major Appleton. On returning he received the Queen's medal for active service together with four clasps insCribecl :—Cape Colony', Laing's Nek, Orange Free State, and Transvaal. He left the battalion in 1902. The funeral took place quietly at the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. PREMIER CYCLES, 7,8, 10, 12 and 14 guineas Cash or 18 monthly payments. Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53 and 55, Bridge-st.—Advt. Warrington Woman's Suicide. Honour Woods, wife of Jno. Woocls,l2, Dolman's- lane, committed suicide early on Wednesday morning. Shortly after nine o'clock a lodger named John Golden, who had been working all night, was retiring to bed when he found her body hanging from the bed- room door. P.C. Wise was called in and the body was at once cut down but life was extinct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Sad Drowning Fatality. A sad drowning fatality has been reported to the police. It appears that on Wednesday morning Arthur Knowles, aged two years, son of Mr. Charles Knowles is, Mill Lane, went for a walk with his sister. When near Buttermilk Bridge, which crosses the Sankey and St. Helens Canal, the lad in play ran away from his sister, and in doing so stumbled and fell into the water The alarm was at once raised but when a man named Job Itudge'appeared on the scene no trace of the child was to be found. Grappling irons were procured and the body quickly recovered, but artificial respiration which was applied by P. C.Plumb was unsuccessful.— The inquest will be held this (Friday) evening.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Wedding.—Critchley —Fleming. A wedding of considerable local interest was cele- brated at Wycliffe , Church, on Thursday, the contracting parties being Miss Sarah Ada Fleming, daughter of Mr. Louis Fleming, St. Mary's-street, J and Mr. Arthur Critchley, son of Mr. Thomas j Critchley, 24, Froghall-lane. There was a j large attendance at the church to witness the J ceremony which was performed by the Rev. John Yon cre. The bride wore a dress of creme voile trimmed with Valenciennes race, and a creme hat to match. She carried a shower bouquet of white roses. The bridesmaids were Miss E. Fleming, Miss E. AshtA, Miss Clara Fleming, and Miss ,Eva Podmore. The first two named were attired in dresses of white voile trimmed with heliotrope flowers, and carried shower boquets of carnations, roses, and sweet peas. The two little. girls wore dresses of white muslin and carried baskets of flowers. Mr. Thomas Critchley acted as best man. Suitable selections, including the well-known Wedding Marches, were played by Mr. T. Wallington. The reception was held at " Raddon Court," Latchford, after which the happy couple left for St. Anne's and Blackpool for the honeymoon, the bride travelling in a grey costume. A large number of handsome Presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Lymm Dam. Sir,—May I be permitted to bring before the notice of your many readers, what is, in my opinion, a gross infringement of public rights. Visiting Lymm about a week ago, I strolled by the side of the dam along that beautiful walk known as" Lyrarn Bongs," but before I had proceeded many yards I found my way obstructed by a fence, which has been recently erected and which for a distance of about a quarter of a mile completely encloses the path and bank by the edge of the dam. Now this walk is Lymm's chief attraction and although the main footpath still remains open, the lower path and bank now enclosed have been for the past 50 years to my own knowledge, quite open to the public. No attempt having been inade during that period to claim them either by fences, notice boards or other means. Under these circumstances it seems to me that the land- lord of the adjoining property has no legal right to erect such a fence and as a regular visitor to Lymni I wish to protest against this obstruction and hope that others who, like myself have enjoyed for so many years the right of way along this lower path, will take up the matter and endeavour to keep both the path and bank open to the public. "FIAT JUSTITIA."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>"Sweated Industries and Drugged Peoples."</head>
        <p>"Sweated Industries and Drugged Peoples."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Under this heading "The Examiner" of last Saturday reported an energetic and powerful discourse by the Pastor of Wycliffe Congregation. Mr. Yonge's utterances were full of truth, well and forcibly spoken, but-somewhat marred by what seemed a libel on his brethren. The drugging and the sweating of which the Pastor complained were laid to the charge of Christian people. Let me at once relieve Mr. Yonge and his fellow believers of such an imputation as this. No loyal follower of Christ who has been taught to love his neighbour as himself could, for his own worldly interest, drug his fellow creature with strong drink and opium, or defraud him of his earnings. It is the men who put on Christianity for a disguise who do these things, and they are to be found in Parliament, in the factory and the workshop, yea, in all the relations of life.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Prefix "Reverend."</head>
        <p>The Prefix "Reverend."</p>
        <p>Sir,--in the "Liverpool Echo" of 25th May last I read that, after laying down his "Doctorate of Divinity," Mr. C. F. Aked,Baptist minister of Pembroke Place Chapel, Liverpool, said in a letter to another newspaper, "I do not want the Papal prefix reverend. I am sure that I am not any more entitled to reverence than the majority of my neighbours, and less entitled than some of them. The name my mother gave me is good enough for me." It is refreshing to see a modern pastor thus frankly release.himself from a false posi- tion, ev'tn although there is not much virtue in the action. It is a pity, bowever,that Mr. Aked did not see his way to favour the public with higher motives for , his recantation" than a mere comparison of himself with his neighbours. No doubt he spoke truly when be disclaimed having any more right to reverend than they had but this was a very low position to take, seeing tnat he had ample materials in "his study" for an exhaustive and convincing repudiation of the prefix in question on Scripture grounds. Let me respectfully step in where the pastor preferred to keep out. In the first place, it is a remarkable fact, worthy of the gravest attention, that the word under discussion i occurs only once in the Bible. Speaking of God n the 9th verse of the cxi Psalm, the writer says, "Holy and Reverend is His name." Now the Almighty having revealed Himself te us as a Being to be revered and worshipped it surely follows that for any man, be be a Bishop, Priest, Deacon, or Pastor, to arrogate to himself one of His attributes is an unpar- donable presumption, if no. indeed an actual sin. God will not have His glory to be given to another. Besides, there is nothing in poor human nature to revere or worship. The very best of men are only creatures of dust and ashes, and so full of infirmities, physioill and mental, that deep humility becomes them ; but self- loritlcation does not. Indeed the smallest possible share of modesty should deter any right-minded man from placing himself on the same plane as his Creator in any way whatever. Then there is the negativik argument to be drawn from the example of the Apostles which must not be over- looked. The Apostles were very humble men, only fishermen on the Lake of Galilee. And yet they were the most remarkable men the world has ever yet seen. While their Lord lived with them He endowed them with power to perform miracles ; and on the day of Pentecost this power was augmented by the gift of tongues. But the fishermen were not puffed up by these wonderful manifestations of the Divine favour ; not one of them ever assumed a title or prefix to adorn his name. And yet, although poor and weak in themselves, they were a mighty power in the hands of God for the salvation of men—a power of which our reverend men seem to know nothing.</p>
        <p>But far more important than all I have yet advanced is the indisputable fact that Jesus himself at once and for ever vetoed worldly honours in connection with the ministry of Ills gospel. If your readers will take the trouble to examine verses 5 to 12, both ;inclusive, of the xxiii. chapter of Matthew's Gospel, and the corresponding marginal references, they will need nothing more to convince them that such pre- fixes as "reverend," " right reverend," and " most reverend father in God" are as directly opposed to the mind and will of Christ as it is possible for anything to be. it is true indeed that the phrases Rabbi, Master, and Father, are the only ones included in the Scriptures referred to because the Scribes and Pharisees had not then presumed to regard themselves as reverend men. But it is with the comprehensive spirit, more than with the letter, of Christ's instructions to His followers, that we have to do. If he considered Rabbi, Mister, Father, inadmissible in a Jewish teacher of religion how strongly would he have condemned the application of reverend to a Christian one:!—Yours truly, June, 1906.</p>
        <p>lON A.</p>
        <p>A young Frenchman naraed Plithon, lying in Guernsey prison under a charge of murder, on Thursday removed the lock of his cell door. gained access to the roof by means of a skylight, and reached an exterior wall and lowered himself to the road by strips of his prison quilt knotted together. Plithon is believed, to have loosened the lock with a nail.</p>
        <p>DOG LICKING THE DEAD MAN'S HAND. At an inquest held at Maidstone on Thursday evening a verdict of " Accidental death" was returned in the case of Lieutenant-Colonel Walter Reginald Rudges, late of the Royal Artillery, who was found dead with!a portion of his face 'blown away on his estate. He had been rabbit shooting was found at the bottom of a slibpery bank with his gun beneath hill], and his retriever dog licking his hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CHRISTIAN RE-UNION. REV. S. PITTS' VIEWS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN RE-UNION. REV. S. PITTS' VIEWS.</p>
