        <p>THE ESSENCE OF DISCIPLESHIP</p>
        <p>---- and it was the only valid bond of fellowship. Whoever could not say "Amen " to that most Christian prayer was himself a schismatic, a ,Dissenter from Christ's own Established Church, and a Nonconformist to Apostolic doctrine and rule. It was not a question nf Anglican and Congregationalist seeing "eye to eye" in respect of forms and ceremonies, or even beliefs and doctrines. So long as the world lasted, never would they" see eye to eye " on such matters, and it was not discoverable anywhere in the New Testament that the Lord desired or required that they should. But He did seek that they should see "eye to eye" in their personal love to Him. When our Lord prayed the Prayer quoted as the text it was Christian unity that he desired, not Christian uniformity. The preacher illustrated how these two expressions did not mean the same thing. Really, he continued, the spectacle presented by some Churches that professed uniformity was a travesty of any idea of unity. There was far more real, essential unity between the varied denominations of which the uniformity churches spoke disparagingly as "sects." Jesus Christ did not form His 'Church on any one exclusive line or in any one exclusive form. The appeal for prayer for the union of Christians gave as one reason for its acceptance that our existing divisions hinder or even paralyze His work." That would seem to possibly suggest that the existence of the different Christian denominations created a hindrance to Jesus Christ. It seemed to him , that that,by itself, was surely a very questionable state- ment. Tt was not the distinct and diverse societies of Christians that made the difficulty, but it was the spirit of division, rivalry, animosity, arrogance, dogmatism, and bigotry which created it. There had been in the history of the Christian Church manifold causes of</p>
        <p>_..isby, . ithington, Manchester, he received his education at the Manchester Grammar School and Owens College: After having been in practice as a medical man in Bury, he obtained the appointment of medical officer to the , Friendly Societies' Medical Association of Warrington and came, to this. town 19 years ago. He had many 'accomplishments, and took a special interest in music, painting, photography and athletics.He was a very clever • pianist and occasionally 'performed at local musical functions where his services were much appreciated. He was also very fond of painting and possessed conz siderable artistic taste. In his .younger days he was 1 an enthusiastic athlete and he did much useful work in connection with the Gymnasium. He was also a Vice-president of the Cycling Club and at one time hon. Secretary of the Warrington Photographic Society. ; Mountaineering .was his favourite holiday pastime and ;he had made several ascents of Mont Blanc. Oil I one occasion, it will be rernembered, he described his experiences in' an interesting article which , appeared in the " Examiner." He had often visited Cumberland and Westmorland to climb the moun- tains there. In politics Dr. Kisby was a staunch Liberal although he never iJok an active part in political work. Fie was a member of the Church of England. He leaves a widow and two children with whom the deepest sympathy is expressed in their 'bereavement.</p>
        <p>BITTER CONTROVERSY, occasions for contending factions, bodies of Christian believers excommunicating one another,and so forth. The age of such great doctrinal and ecclesiastical conflicts seemed to have largely gone by. But they had left some of their spirit behind them ; and it was that narrow and litigious spirit which made difficulty and fostered separation and aloofness between Christians to-day. What was needed was the uprising and growth of a better spirit—the spirit of mutual confidence and brotherly sympathy. It might strike some perhaps that this appeal for unity appeared at a most unpropitious moment. The hosts, at least on one side of a controversy,were being marshalled for conflict, and the harangues of leaders were couched in terms that savoured little of conciliation and compromise. Tt should, however, not be without a pacifying influence that the</p>
        <p>FL tG OF TRUCE should be seen waving even though in the background on the very scone of conflict. The appeal of Arch- bishops and Bishops for penitence and prayer and peace should have some deterrent effect upon the militant host which their own Unguarded and ungoverned zeal had hurried into war. Both sides should try to believe that there was conscience on both sides. Hard names. offensive charges and counter charges should be stayed. In the sacred name of religion in which both sides more or loss believed,onl v truth should be spoken.only right should be striven for. peaking quite candidly, continued Mr. Yonge,he did not thiiik the difficalty about the Union of Christians in fellowship was .as great with the Non- conformist churches as it was with the Established. He did not think it was with them so tremendous a problem as it was especially with thesacerdotal Anglican. There- fore he hailed this appeal from the latter with all the greater gladness and hope. It indicated the growth of a desire towards fellow Christians who were not of their communion or of their form of faith which was as new as it was beautiful. In conclnsion Mf.Yonge said: In measure as you and I sympathize with our Lord's own desire and prayer,we shall reyoice in this"token for good." And we shall join in this concert of prayer. We shall even try to make this petition for unity among the followers of Jesus Christ part of our daily private devotion. For if it becomes part of our accustomed prayer, sincerely, it will come to be also part of our growing habit of thought about fellow Christians of other Churches, and of our conduct and speech about them. " DiSPOSitiOng may thus be created and nourished which will do much to end the needless embitterment so frequently pervading our differences of judgment and creed, and a.fret3r way will be prepared for the divine purpose to realize itself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>"Unity Without Uniformity."</head>
        <p>"Unity Without Uniformity."</p>
        <p>DEATH OF DR. KISBY.</p>
        <p>TUE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the cemetery this (Friday) afternoon, many relatives and friends attending to pay a last tribute of respect. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton. officiated. The chief mourners were :—Mrs. Kisby (widow). Miss Connie gisby (daughter), Mrs. Jones. of Guilsfield, (mother-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. Frost, of Man- chester (brother-in-law and sister), Mr. B. Gittens, Mr G. (litter's, Mr. Derry Kisby. Mrs. Gittens. Mr. Ethelston (cousins), Miss Eva Gittens, Nurse' Lloyd, Dr. Neden, Dr. Binns. Mr. Turner and Mr Harry Lloyd. The following officers of the Warrington Amalgamated Friendly Societies' Medical Association attended :—Mr. Samuel Dimbobbin (president), Mr. H. Blackshaw (vice-president),Mr.Peter Edgar (treasurer), Messrs. George Roughley, William Barlosv,and William Bibby. The Liberal Club was represented by Mr. T. Pemberton, Mr. J. T. Locker, and Mr. JohnWorrall, and the Cycling Club by Mr. E. Pickavance and Mr. Arthur Booth. The following acted as bearers:—Messrs. W. Brown, Robert Perdue, Robert Lees, Arthur Booth, J. Podruore, and John Bibby. Amongst those sending floral tributes were the following :—His wife and children ; his mother ; Mrs. Jones (of Bod Issa. Guils- field) ; Mr. and Mrs. Frost and family ; Mrs. George Kisby and Son (St. Ives) ; Mr. and - Mrs. W. Bibby; Dr. Binns and family ; Messrs. J. and B. Podmore ; Messrs. Reginald and Cecil Joseph: Mrs. Gittens and family : Mr. and Mrs. Russell (Huntingdon) Mr. and Mrs. Robert Lees; Mr. Wm. Brown ; Nurse E. Lloyd; Committee of the Warrington Friendly Societies, Medical Association: Warrington Football Club (of which Dr. Kisby was for many years the medical officer) ; LiberarClub ; Cycling Club; Druids Hope Lodge and Druids' Camp Lodge of the Order of Druids.</p>
      </div>
