      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Personal. , "The Argus," Melbourne, states that Mary Eleanor Edelsten has obtained the degrees of Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery at the Melbourne University. Messrs. Scott Ward, T. Harrison of Fearnhead, and Robert Bold of Croft—have all passed the preliminary examination as lay preachers in connection with the Independent Methodist Church, These three gentle- men are all members of the Risley Methodist Church. PUBLIC BENEFIT, TINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRIL—AdVt. Labourer Knocked Down by a Cab. John Goulding, aged 50, a labourer at Dallam Farm, has been admitted into the Infirmary suffering from an injury to his back. It was stated that he was crossing Wbawick-street near the Central Station when he, was knocked down by a cab. Messrs. Crosfield's Band. At the musical and athletic festival which was held at Shrewsbury on Monday, Wingates Temperance Band, Bolton, gained premier honours in the °nen band _contest, with Crosfield's and Royal 0 ikeley (Festiniog) equal second. Crosfield's won the quickstep, com- petition, Litherland being second. A Warrington Band at Chester. On Saturday evening the Warrington Volunteer Band, conducted by Mr. J. T. Richardson, opened the musical season in Grosvenor Park, Chester, with the following selections :—March, " 0.H.M.5." (Hume); overture, " Latona " (Hain); naval fantasia, "A. sailor's life," (Cope); duet, " Excelsior" (Balfe), descriptive fantasia, " A trip to Blackpool" (Bidgood); march, "The Madcap," (Sharp) ; and selections from Sullivan's operas.</p>
        <p>SIZ-GUINE:k CYCLF.S. Excellent value. Call and inspect.—J. S. GanLatin,sl to 55, Bridge-st.—Advt. Interesting Presentation. On Sunday last the members of the St. George's Men's Bible 'Class pre.ented Mr. Gerald Stunner with a beautiful oak timepiece on the occasion of his. marriage to Miss J. Buckley, youngest daughter of Mr. Joseph Buckley of the Prince of Wales Hotel. The Rely. T. A. J. Ridpath in making the presentation on behalf of the class expressed the hope that Mr. Sumner would have a long and prosperous married life and that he would not sever his connection with the work be had so ably fulfilled in the past. Mr. Turton also expressed the pleasure it gave all the members to make the presentation and congratulated Mr. Sumner on his forthcotning marriage. The recipient replied and thanked the members heartily for their kindness towards him.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. Society. The weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. Mr. W. Lighl presided over a fair attendance and delivered a shot.% address. He quoted a saying of Horade Bushnell's, " God has a plan for every man, and as many plans as there are men in the world" and pro- ceeded to urge all preseut to seek out " God's plan" and then carry it out with all their might. Mr. W. Drewett pointed out what a high conception vie all should have of the worship of God. Miss D. M. Lawton rendered two violin solos in a very effective manner. Miss Oakley and Mr. J. Pownall were the accompanists. Mr. J. D. Gibson read the Scripture lesson, and Mr. E. J. Jones also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>• LAWN MOWERS, Garden Tools, esc. Splendid selection.-3. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-st.—Advt. Escape and Recapture. At Warrington County Sessions, on Wednesday James Higham, a Warrington labourer, was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour for being on the enclosed premises of Messrs. Lawson and Sons at the Corporation water-works, Croft, for an unlawful, purpose. At four o'clock on the morning of the 6th tilt, the prisoner, in company with another man, was seen by Police-constable Flannery moving about the works in a cautious manner. The men approached the shed in which the officer was concealed, and both ran away, but were captured. Prisoner escaped, but the other man was rearrested and sentenced to a term lof imprisonment. Prisoner was not seen again until I the 22nd ult., when he was reanested at Warrington.</p>
        <p>BABY, CARRIAGES. Latest patteros.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Agricultural Shows.—Local Successes. The annual show in connection with the Orraskirk and Southport Agricultural Society was held at South- ; port on Wednesday. In addition to specials, prizes to ; the value of £450 were offered, and for these 1,555 exhibits were entered. In the cattle classes the first 1 prize in the section for a bull over one and under two 1 years of age, was won by W. Norcott, Warrington.l Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. carried off the first prize ; with a magnificent boar in ;he first of the pig classes.l In the poultry section the premier award for a cock or 1 hen went to W. A. Veen, of Lymm. The Shropshire; and West Midland Agricultural Society held their annual show on Wednesday, when J. Gould,of Lymm, ' figured with success in the shire horse section. He carried off two firsts for a mare or gelding foaled in 1903, and fora stallion foaled in 19Q4. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER I THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Homing Club Race. Thirty members sent 235 birds for the Bournemouth old birds race in connection with the Warrington F.C. These were liberated by S. Taylor at 8.5, N. wind. Result: —Laycock and Blackshaw 933.4, *Crank Bros. 935.9, '''Rothwell 921.2, *Bothwell 920.8, Burns 913.0, Craven 912 9,1-Crank Bros. 912.8, Hatton and East- wood 912.1, Brooks 911.1, Jackson and Goulden 903.5, Hughes 906.1, Brooks 904, Mason and William 903, Hatton and Eastwood 897, Malkin 891, Caddock 893, Hatt n 837, tßothwell 878, Jackson and Goulden 878, Carpenter 871, Hatch 870, Williams 858, Bracegirdle 836, Pomfrrtt and Belton 831, Ray 822, Mather 819, Irlam 816, Perry and Scot 806, Clark Bros. 804, Stringfellow 791, Wright and Son 781, Healey 768, Boden 766, Clare 747. Laycock and Blackshaw win Mr. H. Clare's special of lOs. 6. 'l'l.d, pool, tfid. pool. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Warrington Male Voice Choirs' Succesa.</head>
        <p>Warrington Male Voice Choirs' Succesa.</p>
        <p>The above choir further added to their laurels on Monday by winning the first prize of £2O and gold medal at the Shrewsbury Annual Eisteddfod which attracted a large entry. Huddersfield, Birmingham, Crewe and Bridgnorth being represented by Male Voice Choirs in addition to our local choir. A new feature of this Eisteddfod was that the adjudicator (Dr. Roland Rogers) was as he facetiously termed it "boxed up "And in his remarks he told the audience .hat be bad been adjudicating twenty-five years and that this was his first" experience, but, that he rather liked it; he got more elbow room and privacy from people who were inquisitive to see what he was writing down and watching his every expression to see how he iiked the performance that was being given. Of one thing they could be sure however and ' this was, that the best choir would get the prize. Ideal weather was enjoyed throughout the day. The choir who turned up in full strength of 50 voices and also a large number of friends journeyed to Shrews- bury in a couple of corridor coaches specially engaged, 3, R. WOOD, Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt.</p>
        <p>retl</p>
        <p>The Forthcoming Cycle Parade. A meeting of the General Committee which has in I hand the arrangements for the forthc-iming cycle I parade was held at the Patten Arms last Friday even- ing. There was a capital attendance, notwithstanding the near approach of the holidays. In the absence of Mr. W. H. Bellian, the chair was occupied by Mr. George Bowman. —Mr. Hope raised the question as to whether the proceeds to be derived from the parade shred be divided between the Infirmary and the ;I Nursing Association, and it was agreed, inasmuch as the extension scheme of the Infirmary was in hand, that the whole of the proceeds should be given to that scheme.—Mr. C. H. Hoult presented the report of the Printing and Advertising Committe,. and said they ; recommended that in the programme there should be inserted photographs of the President and Vice- President, the Chairman, two Secretaries; Treasurer, the late Mr. C. Davies, and the Rev. H. V. Pigot. The i Novelties Committee's report, which was given by Mr. ! Baines, recommended a dram for a bicycle to be fol- lowed up by a large watch competition. The recom, Mendations of the Prizes Committee were presented ;by Mr. J. J. Pritchard, and they invited officials of various Sunday schools to provide as many collectors as possible. Considerable discussion took place with regard to the judges, and this question was .deferred until the next meeting of the committee. All the : recommendations were approved by the General • Committee.</p>
        <p>Warrington second were also victorious, Newton being defeated by 47. A feature of the match was the splendid batting of R. H. Carson who amassed 55 for Warrington. He was at the wicket for an hour and his chief hits included one six and five fars. For the losers F. Harrison also wielded the willow in Igrand style_ and made 5. Bennett and Ashton did I the most Oleot tve work with the ball</p>
        <p>Great interest is maintained in the Warrington Works Competition, but the match between Cros- field's and Locos on iTaesday evening was of the nature of a farce. The soapery eleven carried all before them and sc3red 116, while their opponents were all out for the miserable total of 18. T. Adler- ton(3l)and E. W. Warren(33) were the most successful batsmen. Dickenson and Gleave, the bowlers, had a rare field day, the former capturing six wickets for 10 runs and Gleave 3 for 8.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists scored a triuMph at the expense of Pemberton, thanks to the bowling of Conway and Cox whose analyses were five wickets f.n. 12 and five for 17 respectively.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's had to admit a severe defeat at the hands of Grappenhall on Saturday. Not one of their players attained double figures and were all dismissed for a paltry 19. Grappenhall then showed what they could do, and registered 103, of' which Carter scored 51. Watson bawled in fine style, taking seven wickets for five runs.</p>
        <p>St. James's defeated the Sacondary school by a majority of 11. There was no brilliant scoring on either side, C. Holt heading the list with 10.</p>
        <p>The "Guardian" eleven have been doing great things of late, accounting successively for Penketh Friends' School and Warrington Hall-Holiday.</p>
        <p>1 Penketh went to West Derby on Saturday and experienced a severe drubbing at the hands of the I second eleven. On Monday they played Garston and the match ended in a draw, Penketh, with three wickets to fall, requiring only 18 to win. Shoobridge batted splendidly and made 60 in the secind innings, ' while H. Davies captured eigbt wickets for 37 runs lin the first innings. Penketh had indeed hard lines in not being victorious.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers had to bow the knee to Bewsey-road Wesloyans who were victors by a margin of 20. For the Wesleyans Jolley took six wickets for 19 runs and Craddock four wickets for ten runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>bowling.</head>
        <p>bowling.</p>
        <p>Connected with our local friendly societies there arel many trundles of repute whilst there is also a large, field of undiscovered talent. Earlier in the season it was suggested in connection with the Oddfellows that an Inter-Lodge League should be formed but it was found impossible to carry out the idea. The proposal has not,however,been dropped and will again come forward, probably with greater success, next year. With a view to gnaging in some measure the possibility of success,a handicap main on a large scale is being organized. This will be open to all oddfellows connected with the , Warrington District of the Nfanchester Unity, and the final details will be arranged next week. Promise is , already forthcoming of the success of the venture and it will be surprising if there is not an entry of 200 or even considerably more.</p>
        <p>Further progress in connection with the first round or the Warrington Works Competition was made on Saturday 4ut this 'week-end the green will not be open to the Works teams and play will therefore, for the time being, have to be suspended. Three matches were decided on Saturday and of these only one was pro ductive of anything aporoaohing excitement. This was the one between Greeuings and Sankey Wire Mills, the wirepullers winning by ten points.</p>
        <p>The proceedings were opened by T.'Poster and W. Smith. The first two ends were divided when Poster went to the front and playing up vrell gave Houghton's the lead by 15-5. Matters were levelled up by 1- Weisby who led throughout in Ws game with James Harrison, and brought the totals to 29-21, Greenings being in arrear. After the sixth end A. Lawton had little chance against A. Cooper who from four all bad a run of eight before Lawton againjgokla wood home. The final score was 15-6.</p>
        <p>--.. Sucllow failed to improve matters from Greenings point of view though he fought well with Graham. The Wire 5/ills trundler had all the running at the outset and in five ends led by 8-0. Sudlow then rdse to the occasion but was unable to continue the effort and lost by six points. A somewhat similar result attended the gains between R. Shaw and G. Rutter, the former after leading 8-1 winning by 15-11. Three more games saw the Wire Mills ahead to the extent of 22 points, when W. Foster and T. Jackson were a ssociated, and Jackson proving the better matt the lead WAS redac ed to 15.</p>
        <p>Houghtons again went to the front when R. Clarke and J. Goalden came together. Clarke set off In a sur- prising fashion and when the score stood at 13-0 Houghtons were somewhat apprehensive. Goalden then got his first wood home and went to 6.13 before Clarke again secured the jack and won, after dividing the following point, by 15-9. Houghton's required two points to win and these 'were quickly added by J. G. IFletcher who got two woods home in the first end, and I his final score of 12-15 gave his team the victory by ten 'points.</p>
        <p>Crosfields No. 1 and No. 3 teams were drawn together and No. 1 easily qualified for the second round. Obtain- ing a lead of 13 on the first game their ultimate advan- tage was one of 82 points. The only men in the losing team to give a good account of themselves were It. Welsby, who recorded the only win for No. 3,D. Higham and J. Rudge. AU the others failed to reach double figures.</p>
        <p>A splendid exhibition was given by •Pearson and Knowles team, who placed to their credit all the points which it was possible for thorn to score, whilst their opponent s. Castle Rubber Co., only totalled 70 between them. The match from beginning to end was all in favour of P. &amp; N., and the only question was as to whether any of their men would lose, a question which was answered in the negative.</p>
        <p>One of the closest matches on record was decided on Saturday between Orford Village and 'Warrington Junior Conservatives. Playing on the Orford green the home team in the singles scorei 17i3 to which the Juniors replied with 151. The doubles were even more hotly contested and one point only separated the teams, this being claimed by Orford who on the day won by eight points. When the return match is played there is little doubt that the Conservatives will handsomely avenge their defeat.</p>
        <p>Some of tte most enjoyable events in local howling circles are tfie periodical mains which are decided over the Liberal Club green. These invariably attract a large number of competitors and excellent contests are witnessed. On Sisturday one of these events was won by A. Lawton after a close fight with C. Nevins.</p>
        <p>• cycling. Last week end the weather was ideal for touring and he Warring ton club's visit to Ellesmere and Llangollen was one of the most successful on rcaord. The week- end was spent at Ellesmere, all the beauty spots in the tdistriot being exploren. The return was made on Mon- day via Llangollen, and the outing was thoroughly enjoyed by the large number taking part.</p>
        <p>On Saturday about a dozen members who could not join the touring party had a run to Lower Peover, and spent a very pleasant few hours at this pretty village.</p>
        <p>There was another impromptu run on Monday, the destination being Oniton Park. The run through Dela- mere Forest was delightful, and at the Park the military fete was watched with great interest and pleasure.</p>
        <p>The roads were again in grand condition for the hib run on Tuesday evening when Tabley—another (popular resort—wos visited.</p>
      </div>
