      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>BABY'S MARTYRDOM.</head>
        <p>BABY'S MARTYRDOM.</p>
        <p>Cruel Eczema Ended by Zam Buk.</p>
        <p>Interviewed recently by a " Huddersfield Examiner reporter, Mr. Ben Kenwortby, of George-street, 'Kirk- burton, near Huddersfield, said . . .</p>
        <p>A fortnight after she was born a large number of small blisters broke out on my second child's head. I tried old-fashioned remedies for thrush, but the blisters developed into running sores. The doctor said it was eczema. He gave me a powder, but it stuck to the sores. increased the irritation, and they spread all over her head and face. The doctor gave me ointment and medicine next, but though I persevered with this treat- mant for ten months no improvement was perceptible. was compelled to tie her hands and cover her bead with a cotton cap. Her sufferings had reduced her td a there shadow, and I myself was little better. A 'nurse's and a chemist's treatment were unavailing. "About this time, I bought a small box of Zam-Buk, appliad the ,halm gently to the sores, and at the end of two days the sores were drying. They gradually healed and when I had used up the box of Zam-Buk hardly a single spot was visible. I used two more boxes, and her skin became as fair as a lily." Zam-Bok ends disease and renews the skin either in infancy or old age.</p>
        <p>--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Successful Warrington Athletes.</head>
        <p>Successful Warrington Athletes.</p>
        <p>Local athletes on Monday competed at various meetings in the country and with considerable success. At the Hospital Sports at Rotherham, on Saturday, B. Gornall, of Warrington, won the half-mile bicycle , handicap off the 75 yards mark, the mile handicap off I the 155 yards mark, and he was third in the three- quarter mile handicap off 130 yards. The second ' prize in the mile went to Charles Berry, of Warrington who started off 70. At the same • meeting W. Price of Warrington' (150) won the mile walking handicap after giving a hundred yards' start to the second and third Prize-winners. At the Oulton Park Fete on IMonday in the half mile open handicap the first prize ' fell to F. Houghton after a god race, E. Richardson, another Warringtoni an, arriving home second. In the open quarter mile the premier honours were carried off by F. Anderton a promising local runner. The Isecond prize in the three mile open handicap at Bangor was carried off by H. K. Jepson Warrington A.C., who Iwas given 225 yards start.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>That tired feeling quickly vanishes after a few nibbles of</p>
        <p>plasmon chocc!ate</p>
        <p>Authorised by H.M. War Office as an Emergency Ration. Brimlui of nourishment, but be sure it's plasmon Chocolate</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN, • Many thanks to you for all the very nice verses of poetry I have received. I see you have all tried to win a prize, and I am sorry I eannot•give every one of you prizes, but this,.of course, is quite impossible. I print a few pieces now, and each week I will insert a little so that you may all see your contributions in print by degrees. I have sent the prize to the mem- ber whose contribution is the first printed in these columns. There was no name attached to this only the adciressoso I hope the prize will reach its destination safely. I think you will agree that it is well worth a prize; I am sure you will all be interested reading it, lam also sending a prize to Faith Mewitt for her very pretty story. I have had this story by me for a long time, so you must not mind if your contributions do not appear for a few weeks.</p>
        <p>I hope you have all started your pin-cushions for our next competition. I dare say some of you will think of different shapes to make them; you can make them very pretty with very small pieces of material. lam glad to wel- come some new members. As you will see, our Corner is growing larger every week. I shall continue, for the present, offering prizes weekly for the best contributions sent me for the "Corner," but these must be Well and tidily written. I see that some of you continue to write, in pencil; of course, I disqualify these. I must say good-bye for this week, so with all good wishes from your affectionate</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>DEAR 'UNCLE NED,—I am sending a piece of poetry, and I hope it will please you. It is called the "Carpenter's Shop." lam eleven years old. THE CARPENTER'S SHOP. One morning a spruce little gimlet Looked into a carpenter's shop, And standing upright on its screw, It surveyed it from bottom to top. "Much company—no conversation!" It said, as it looked at the tools; "All standing stock still in their places! They must be a parcel of fools."</p>
        <p>"Are you well I" said the spruce little Gimlet, Addressing itself to the Plane. " Pretty well, when I'm well fed on shavings, That are not too coarse in the grain."</p>
        <p>"And you, do you like your vocation?" "'Tie wearisome work," said the Saw, "To gnaw all day long at hard timber, It gives one a pain in the jaw." So then it inquired of the Hatchet, /That hung with its sharp-looking nose Hooked over a peg in the wall, If it "liked dealing'out heavy blows?" The Hatchet vouchsafing no answer, The Gimlet turned round on its screw, And said to a great heavy Mallet, "That question's intended for you." "I always was told," said the Mallet, "To look at my friends when I spoke'; My head aches a good deal this morning, It suffers from every stroke." "Will you dance?" Gimlet asked of the Pincers; "I see you're provided with legs, Though I can't cdmpliment you upon them— A couple of qtfeer-looking pegs!" Ndtwithstanding, the Pincers were flattered, And straddling across a deal board, They slid from the top to the bottom, Without even speaking a word. The Gimlet turned merrily, round On its sharp little screw of a leg, While the Pincers made many a bound And a pirouette, poised on one peg. The Plane and the Saw and the Mallet ' Made music—each such as it could; And the whole joiner's shop mug with</p>
        <p>_ laughter That pe.aled from the unseasoned *rood.</p>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS. '</p>
        <p>Fred Roundel], Hannah Rushton, Annie Barnes, Edward Royds, May Briggs, Martha Walmersley, N. Riley, F. Burtwistle, E. Briggs, R. Jackson, N.. Turner, M. Tecklow, E. Wilcock, L. Hindle, G. Wilcock, J. Pilling, J. Wilcock, A. . Smith, M. Haworth, R. Whittaker, F. Haworth, W. Whittaker S. Holden, L. Fenwick, Flank ,DalEs, John C. SWinn, Charles Midwood, 'Cecil Taylor, Arthur Parker.</p>
        <p>Flitting o'er a meadow, Went two butterflies. One of simple yellow, One of many dyes. Passing near each other, Said bright and gay, "You look nothing, brother, In that plain array!" Scarce had this been uttered, When a boy came past, Chased the many coloured Till he held him fast Still the yellow Safely , O'er the meadow fled, Thanking fate that made Ulm Without gold or red.</p>
        <p>[OW FILIPPE'S COW WAS SAVED.</p>
        <p>During the war of 1870, when the Geri troops were marching on Paris, Filippe Lero a young French girl, had a pet cow, Fauch( which was almost the only support of family. To save her from the Germans she was, ur the care of Filippe, allowed to graze onl] night, and kept hidden in the daytime, here we commence the story.</p>
        <p>It was Filippe's task every niight, as soon as she had cleared away the supper things, to come with Fauchette to the meadow where she grazed, and stand guard over her for the hours necessary to give her sufficient time for her meal. /It was a lonely and dreary vigil, and many times Filippe felt her heart sink.</p>
        <p>One night when the new moon gave just enough light to make out objects clearly, Filippe was suddenly startled by the sound of many horses' feet coming along the road. It needed no glance in that direction to know that a body of horsemen were approaching at a slow gallop. With the swiftness of the wind, she flew to Fauchette's side, and with her hand on -the gentle creature's shoulder, was about to push her away toward a clump of tall bushes. But, alas! through some carele...s- ness the bell had not been removed, and either it gave out a tinkling sound at this -moment, or Filippe's swift running had drawn attention to herself. At any rate, before she could move the cow, a gruff voice called Out to her in her own tongue, "Hold /on there! We see you! Do,n't be trying to get that cow away!" Overcome with -terror, Filippe could only stand with her hand against the cow's shoulder, looking in the direction whence the gruff voice had 'come. The soldiers had now halted. Some had al- ready dismounted, and were climbing the fence. Others tore a wide gap and entered through it on their horses. How their guns and sabres glittered in the moonlight! " Oh, Fauchetter exclaimed Filippe, with a ' burst of tears, as she threw her arms round ' her dear cow's neck, "I am afraid they are going to kill you!" Then with a sudden determination, she cried, "But if they do, they'll have to kill me first!"</p>
        <p>"Let go that cow, girl I" said the same harsh voice, now unpleasantly near to her. "We must have her." , "What do you want of her?" said Filippe, raising her head at this point, and wonderiug at her own bravery. "To eat!" was the gruff response. At these words Filippe burst into tears. To Eat! Her beautiful Fauchette I No, no, no! "Are you going to get away or not?" the Man questioned again. "If you do nqt, I'll , have you taken away by force. Come!" She did not move, but stood with her arms firmly clasped about the neck of Fauchette. The officer turned to two of the men who had dis- mounted, and were standing near. "Seize her!" he said.</p>
        <p>They started to obey orders. Filippe saw them coming, and her screams rang far and near. They were echoed by an angry exclama- tion from the direction of-the road, and the next moment a horseman on a powerful horse came galloping through the gap into the field. He was a mail. in the prime of life, with an air that bespoke the commander. Under his glance the men who had been about to seize Filippe slunk away. Only the officer held his ground, and he dropped his head confusedly. "What does this mean?" sternly demanded the newcomer. But he didn't wait for an answer.</p>
        <p>"There little girl, do not cry," he said, in the kindest tones, "they shall not take your cow. Return home with her. It is late for a little girl like you to be out." The while Filippe, smiling through her tears and courtesying, drew Fauchette away, the C-onamander turned to the men, and she could hear him, after she had. gone some distance, angrily reprimanding the soldiers. Filippo did not know until long afterwards that the noble-looking horseman who had come just in time to save her dear Fauchette was no less a personage than Frederick William, Crown Prince of Germany—the Good."Unser Fritz " who died only a few yeans ago, so universally loved and regretted. F. MEWETT. Address wanted.</p>
        <p>, • DEAR TJistorx NED,—Please accept my_ little contribution. I hope you are quite wed. I am glad to hear that your birthday is'three days before mine. You will-be glad to hear that I was top of my last term examination. What beautiful spring weather we are having. I am very busy now, as I am going in for the County Scholarship Examinktion on June 20. I am sending my favourite little piece of poetry. With love and best wiehes, I remain, your loving niece,</p>
        <p>GLADYS KNICIIIT. FORGET ME NOT. When verdure first bedecked the earth In Eden's happy bowers, The Lord, 'tis said, at cool of day, Came down amongst the flowers. To each he gave a fitting name. To each a word of love; And blessed the garden He had made Fair as the heaven above, Again He came at close of day, And walked among the flowers; But.one He saw, a blossom fair, Was sad in Eden's bowers. The loVing Lord bent tenderly, And raised its dfooping head; When, "Lord, my name I have forgot," The blue-eyed blossom said.</p>
