      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Cyclist Abroad</head>
        <p>The Cyclist Abroad</p>
        <p>has more facilities than perhaps any other class of traveller for seeing a great variety of life, costume, architecture, scenery.—in fact, everything that people go abroad to see can be seen under better and more natural conditions if the traveller is a cyclist.</p>
        <p>Holland is a favourite touring ground of many cyclists, and the four days of the Bank holiday week ends afford ample time for an entertaining tour among the -unaccustomed sceriety and strange costumes of the Netherlands.</p>
        <p>. . For many years past no district abroad has been more popular as a teuring-ground for cyclists bent on enjoying a brief holiday than the Belgian Ardennes. , The attractions of the country I.:nib:vice peaceful pastoral scenery, fine forests. good roads, very cheap inns, and: excellent facilities for travelling to and from this country. Namur is the customary, starting-point, and Cologne forms a convenient :terminal. The great cave of Han, with its Mammoth stalactites and stalagmites thoroughly &amp;servos visiting. It quite transcends in its subterranean immensity anything to ho seen in this country. At Treves, with its Holy Coat, a variety of Roman antiquities delight the visitor, and if at Echternach during Whitsun- tide the tourist may see an amazing procession, when the whole countryside' headed by the pries, hops solemnly through the town to avert cattle plague for the year.</p>
