      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <p>1</p>
        <p>DRUIDS IN CONFERENCE. --- Grand Master Houghton on Urgent Questions.</p>
        <p>The annual delegation of the Order of Druids assembled on Tuesday in the Rotherham Town Hall Assembly-rooms.. Mr. David Houghton, of Warrington, Grand Master, presided. There was a full attendance of delegates, numbering 108, with a voting strength of 51,242 members. The Grand Master, in his opening address, said that, lhaving regard to the general depression in trade, the progress of the Order had been -fairly satisfactory, the total membership being now 71,242, an Increase on the year of I,s6o.Financially their fosition was equally, satis- factory, the funds having increased by £10,664. He was glad to observe that the interest received on invest- ments showed a substantial advance. This showed that more care and attention was being paid to the char- acter and stability of their investments, and he hoped \to see a further advance in the same direction. Referring to the questions discussed at the National Friendly Societies' Conference the Grand Master said it was desired that action should be taken to prevent periodi- cal divisions of capital and exemption from quin- quennial valuations. Another question was that of the injurious effect which the operation of the Workmen's Compensation' Act was having upon the funds of friendly societies.. One thing was certain--that since the Workmen's Compensation Act cgrne into force friendly societies had experienced a heavier sick rate—and be was quite sure that, whether it had caused more malingering or not, it bad caused a certain glass of members to declare on the funds of their societies, who probably never would have done so if it had not been for the benefits under the Act. The societies had to pay foi the two weeks for which the employer was not liable. fie feared that they would he compelled, ih order to maintain their financial stability, to adopt some !medial measure. The importance of concerted action on the part of all the friendly societies was apparent. The alternatives were increased subscriptions or reduced benefits, or that when the member became entitled to compensation under the Act his sick benefit should cease: It was also proposed that compulsory powers should be obtained for all registered friendly societies to bo valued upon the same basis as regarded sickness and mortality experience. The leakage that (lectured in their member- ship from year to year was yet another subject of inquiry, the number of members who left • the various societies through suspension or lapses • being estimated at 110,000 annually. He thought the reason was to be found in the delaying of full benefit for so long a periodl as twelve months. Life insulance companies granted immediate henefit,and he hoped to see friendly societies adopting some measures in that direction. Any member leaving a society, he felt convinced, became an active agent against it, and so increased the evil. Their fathers worked in the en:use of thrift and mutual help in great darkness and discouragement.yet they reared the fabrics which they were proud to believe had proved an immense force in the uplifting of the masses, and surely in these days of compare ti ye light and recog,nition and encourage- tnhi eant twthhiecyh shhaodu lbde snob lwe etiol bme galt a i (nA papnlda u sset lig then Business was suspended at noon. The delegates accepted the Mayor's invitation to lunch and afterwards in brilliant weather, drove to Wentworth Woodhouse,the home of Lord Fitzwilliam.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday Mr. G. ff. Hall (Sheffield), the Vice. Grand Master, was unanimously elected Grand Master, Mr. R. V. Clayton (Manchester) Grand Treasurer, and Mr. John Westall (Manchester) Grand Secretary, were re-elected with applause. For the office of Vice Grand Master there were four candidates—Messrs. C.W.Shipp (Bristol), J. W. Stickney (Leeds), W. Peter Turner (Hanley), and T. W. Grundy (Rotherham). On the first vote, l‘lessrs. Shipp and Grundy obtained large majorities, but neither secured au actual majority on the voting strength. A second vote was then taken,with the result that Mr. Shipp secured election by a sub- stantial margin.' For three vacant seats on the board of management there were five candidates—Messrs. William Brown (Manchester). J. F. G. Brown (Stock- ton-on-Tees), Albert Wilde (Sheffield). Philip Jones (oswestry(, and N. E. Whilbb (Leeds). On the first vote, Messrs. William Brown, Wilde, and Whilhy obtained the requisite majorities, and were declared duly elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>RE-DISCOVERED FOOD.</head>
        <p>A RE-DISCOVERED FOOD.</p>
        <p>An important discovery has recently been made in connection with food. The most nutritious and least expensive is currant bread. The King's physician recommends it, and would like to see it ou the table in this country at every mes,l. Cprraut bread should be cut in thin slices to obtain the full nutriment and flavour. Leading bakers will supply White or Hovis currant bread.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>RECORD.</head>
        <p>A RECORD.</p>
        <p>The tea trade has recently created a "customs " record in the payment of a duty cheque upwards of £lll,OOO, but it Is for the wine trade of our Colonies to eclipse all preceding trade records. The fact has just been established, by basing the figures on Govern- ment statistics, that P. B. Burgoyne and Co. Ltd., paid duty during 1905 upon one twenty-eighth of the entire imports of all wines (retained for home consumption) into the United Kingdom. This means that one bottle of wine in 3very twenty-eight consumed is Burgoyne's Australian.</p>
      </div>
