      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday morning the preacher was the Rev. Sidney Pitt who, before the sermon, spoke at some length on the question of Christian reunion. The subject' of his sermon was "Sunday observance." At the evening service the Rev. J. Manning preached on: "The law of cause and effect in human ammeter," his text being : "Be not deceived; God is not mocked ; for whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap." (Galatians vi. 7.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Renovation of St. Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>Renovation of St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>" After many hours of conisderation " says the vicar and wardens of St. Paul's Church, in a °hauler which they have issued "we have now commenced the renova- tion scheme, which you are well aware has been found absolutely essential. It is with the greatest possible pride we look back upon the results or your past self- denying efforts for the church you love so well, and therefore this gives us confidence in issuing this appeal for the work now put in hand. Towards the required amount we have during the past six months, by means of special voluntary collections, self-denial boxes and a sale of work, raised the sum of £3OO. Our choir and Bible classes have each promised to contribute £2O within the next twelve months. The scholars of our Sunday schools are hoping, by means of monthly free- will offerings of one penny per child, to provide the sum of £5O within the same period. We further antici- pate raising £lOO through special offertories. From these particulars you will see that young and old are making most strenuOus efforts to restore and beautify the House of God, and we have confidence in asking you to help us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Methodist Anniversary. Visit of gr. Robert Cameron M.P.</head>
        <p>Methodist Anniversary. Visit of gr. Robert Cameron M.P.</p>
        <p>The Sunday School Anniversary in connection with the Alethodist Free Church, Buckley-street, was cele- brated on Sunday when special services were held. Throughout the day these were well attended and in the evening when the service was held in the Parr Hall, there was a crowded congregation. The morning service was held in the church 'in Buckley-street, the pulpit being occupied by Mr. R. Cameron, M.P., who dealt with the religious education of children. Mr. Saunders read the lesson. In the afternoon, in the Parr Hall, a special musical service was held, Mr. John Upson in the chair. The speaker was the Rev. Sidney Pitt, superin- tendent of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit. Special hymns were rendered by the children and choir, and selections given by the Apollo Prize Male Voice Choir, under the directorship of Mr. IL Berrey, and were much appreciated. The evening service was also held in the Parr Hall, when special anthems were given by the children and choir, Mr. Nathan Cook conducting. Mr. E. J. Guest presided, and also read the lesson, while Miss M. Plinston and Mr. A. E. Trow supplied the accompani- ments on the organ and piano. Miss Plinston also effectively gave a solo. „</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Cameron was again the preacher basing his remarks on the text "And besides this, giving all diligence, add to your faith virtue, and to virtue knowledge, and to khowleclge temperance, and to temperance patience, and to patience godliness, and to godliness brotherly kindness and to brotherly kindness love," he said that he wished to show what the religious ellucation of men and women ought to be. In the text they were given eight different subjects to try to attain as they grew in lrfe and developed. Had they been fighting the battle with weakness and growing stronger with ignorance and growing in mental power? Had they been fighting with the craving and instincts of their lower natures until they could say to passion "I am master and I have learnt to control you?" Those were questions which were put to them,' and which they must answer. Man had to grow morally and intellectually in order to become a ilower in the world. It had a taunt against Christians, and perhaps rightly, that they i were no better than other men. Christianity demanded of them. that their word must be their bond, their bargain to be trusted, their sympathy to be relied upon, and their civic, duties to be discharged as became a citizen, and a man in all relations of life. They must !have Christianity not on Sundays only, but in their daily inter- course and dealings with men so that they might resist evil and oppression in every form they must not be cowardly and shrink from duty because it was hard and because it would cost them something, but accept the consequences calmly as Christ did, who died for them on the Cross. Let them forgive their enemies. Christ did not shirk either the Cross or its shame or pain. Let them be true unto death. He hoped they would pardon his being plain but they were not meant to be blockheads. They were meant to be intellectual to grow in knowledge as well as in grace to add truth to truth, principle to principle science to : science, to go on adding that the brain should become a tabernacle of knowledge. Was that not better than that-they should be silly frivolous gossips only desiring to read the latest paragraphs in a gossipy newspaper. He sometimes had to warn young men against the temptations of the day, sport and physical excitement. He admitted that they must develop the body, train every muscle, harden the nerve, develop the strength of the heart, and thus they would become better men in every way. Their frame would act better, their imagina- tion would be healthier and more vivid, but what was the end'of it ? What if they stopped with the phsyical enjoyinent ? What if at the end of 20 years they had not learnt the art of their profession, and had learnt , nothing' of the world? If it had been all sport, one would be a poor creature at 40 Years of age, poor when he ought to be rich. Muscle was healthy and right, buti If they had neglected the higher for the sake of the ' lower it was the highest folly of which they could be guilty in this life, and it would be difficult to amend it. Then to all this they mustedd brotherly love. " God is love," and He must be followed by love. They must be prepared to make heroic sacrifices for the sake of humanity and use their brains not to return evil for evil, and to pay people back in their own coins, but to be true followers of Christ and like Him to smile upon and forgive their enemies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>KITES IN OLD LONDON.</head>
        <p>KITES IN OLD LONDON.</p>
        <p>En writing of the "Birds of London" it is only reasonable to include some references to those which, although they have partially or entirely. disappeared, come within the title of virtue of historical tradition. ,The kite I have already spoken of as a former frequenter ' of London streets,for it was protected by law on account of its public services at a time when sanitary authorities were unknown. Whether the London of the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries were more filthy in condition than the cities of the Continent it is difficult to say,but certainly the fact that foreigners who visited us at that time were struck by the number of these useful Nature's scavengers would appear to be an argument that it was so. One Schaschek, from Bohemia, stated that he had never seen so many kites as around London Bridge, and about a century later an eminent naturalist, Belon, wrote in his "Observations that in London they were almost as numerous as they were in Cairo, and that he saw them not only feeding on the garbage of the streets but even on the flotsam and jetsan,i of the Thames. The last nest of the kite in London was destroyed in 1777 from the trees in Gray's Inn Gardens, when the young hirds were sacrificed on the altar of inquisitive science in order to discover on what they had fed.—From 'The Birds af London, Past and Present," in the " Cornhill Magazine " for June.</p>
        <p>• CONIATERCIAL FAILURES..According to " liemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, June Ist, was ISt, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 101; Deeds of Arrangement registered,Bo. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 84 ; Deeds of Arrangement 79—total 163 ; being an increase of 18. The total num- ber of Commercial Failures recorded during the 22 weeks of the present, year is 3,725 ; the total number recovied in the corresponding 22 weeks of last year was 3,954, showing a decrease of 2:29.</p>
      </div>
