      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>RESOLUTION WITHDRAWN.</head>
        <p>RESOLUTION WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>,late Company and Cement Contracts.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil was held last (Thursday) evening, the Mayor (Alder- man Bolton) presiding. There were also present : Aldermen Bennett, Smethurst, Tinnion, Evans, and Roberts, Councillors Forshaw, Hulse, Chantler, Maginn' NS ilson, Ashcroft, Skelland, Barton, Fulton Smith. Pemberton, Sankey, Clegg, Benson, Stott, Ball, Dicken- son, Brough, Burton, Evans, Dr. Joseph : Messrs. A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), J. Guest Gornall (medical officer of health), T. Longdin (borough sur- veyor), J. Moore Murray (director of education), F. G. Ruddock (borough analyst).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>MAYOR'S BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>The MAYoa announced that as the meeting happened to have been arranged on his birthday, he would have pleasure in asking the members to drink his health at the close. IMPROVEMENT TO PARR, HALL. MR. CHARLTON PARR'S,OFFER.</p>
        <p>The MAvort said he had pleasure in reporting that he had received a letter from Mr. J. Charlton Pair offering to cover at his own expense the side entrance to the Parr Hall, as the door at present was very inconvenient to the artistes and others using the hall. He moved that the Council accept Mr. Parr's kind offer and that the best thanks of the Council be accorded to Mm. The improvement would certainly be a valuable one. (Hear, hear). Alderman SMETHURST seconded and the motion was unanimously carried. THE COMPLETION OF THE POLICE BUILDINGS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>CHANGES OF THE FORCE.</head>
        <p>CHANGES OF THE FORCE.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee resolved that P.C. (61) Bennett (Driver and Curator of Horse Ambulance) be granted the Special Service Badge, carrying with it an increase of ls. per week. A letter was read from Mr. C. W. Davenport, asking when the Committee proposed to allow him to finish his contract in connection with the Police premises.— It was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be authorised to give instructions to Mr. Davenport to commence the completion of his contract. A letter was read from the Secretary of State for the Home Department, asking for the views of the Watch Committee on the question of police supervision over cotton mills in order to prevent time-cribbing.— Resolved that the Home Secretary be informed that this work, in the opinion of the Committee, is entirely outside the province of the Police Force and would be better done by the Factory Inspector. Resolved that P.C.'s Cox and Holland be granted a gratuity of 10s. each for meritorious conduct at a tire in Buttermarket-street. Sergeant Hollingworth having given notice of his desire to retire from the Police Force after upwards of 26 years service and the Chief Constable having certified the service as approved service for the purpose of the Police Acts, 1839 to 1893; it was resolved that there be paid out of the Police Pension Fund to Mr. Enoch A. Hollingworth a pension for life equal to two- thirds of his annual pay; that Acting-Sergeant Condcliffe be promoted to the sank of 2nd class Sergeant ; that P.C. Stuait be promoted to the rank of Acting-Sergeant and Court Officer. Councillor CHANTLER thought the word "commence" was very significant. He thought the job was com- menced long since. The town had been the laughing stock of the country with regard to the police buildings. If they had been erecting Pyramids on the Tower of Babel it might have been different. The worst was that when finished the building would not be Et for the job for which it was intended. He suggested that they should offer the buildings to the Government for a world's fair or something of that description. He would like to know whether they would all be grand- fathers before the work was finished. (Laughter.) The Ma yOR explained that the Committee had been waiting for the money and having got that were satis- fied that they could now go on with the work. The minutes were approved. THE "MOTOR NUISANCE."</p>
        <p>SLATE COMPANY AND CEMENT CONTRACTS• The Paving and Sewerage Committee resolved, (1) That in the opinion of this Committee the serious attention of His Majesty's Government should be drawn to the terrible nuisance and damage caused by motor vehicles in this district ; (2) That this Committee is of opinion that all motor vehicles should be in some measure further taxed, and that a grant from the revenue so derived should be made towards the expense of watering the roads in every district used by motor vehicles in transit to other districts with a view of reducing the vexatious and damaging nuisance caused by the dust, the aforesaid damage and nuisance parti- cularly affecting the farmers' crops for a considerable distance from such roads and also other property immediately adjoining such roads ; (3) That this Com- mittee is of opinion that the cost of preventing the damage so caused should not be borne by the local rates, but by the owners of the motor vehicles ; ( Further, that serious danger accrues to persons using the high- ways from the motor vehicles raising such great clouds of dust as render the sight of other vehicles almost impossible to give time to get out of the way. The Town Clerk was instructed to forward copies of the above resolutions to the President of the Local Govern- rnent Board and the Member for the Borough. The following letter to the Chairman of the Com- mittee (Alderman Tinnion) from the Warrington Slate Co. Ltd. was read :—Dear sir,—We have received a specification for Portland cement required by your Committee, which states that the cement must be supplied from one of the three manufacturers named therein. We respectfully submit that this restrictive condition is certain to work to the prejudice of the Corporation, because it shuts out from competition 14one of the largest sources of supply. We would suggest that the better plan would be to ask for prices of best Portland cement, which shall comply with the British Standard Specification in all respects. This specification is one which is DOW being inserted in nearly all public works contracts because of its string- ency, and can be complied with by a large number of manufacturers, the exallence of whose productions is beyondcontest. The insert on of manufacturers' names in specifications, in regard to any article where it is unnecessary, creates a monopoly to the advantage of the producer and at the cost of the consumer. The final effect of the restriction, if persisted in, will be to deprive local ratepayers of any chance of competing for the town's business. We do not think that the members of your Committee will willingly be parties to a course of procedure which would unquestionably be an injustice to the ratepayers generally, by forcing up prices, and to others in particular by depriving them of a chance to compete. With respect, we beg to remain.—Yours faithfully, (The Warrington Slate Co. Ltd.), R. M. Roemers. The Committee, after carefully considering the matter, decided to continue the policy which they had adopted, and gave instructions for the following letter to be sent to the Warrington Slate co., Ltd., in reply : Dear Sirs,—With further reference to your letter of the 7th instant, ram directed to state for your informa- tion that the committee considered the matter very carefully before coming to their decision, and gave the fullest consideration to all the points raised in your letter. This step was taken because of the great diffi- culty the Corporation have found in obtaining cement of unform quality from local dealers. I must also point out to you that this condition is not so restrictive as you say, and this was explained by the Chairman (Mr. Alderman Tinnion) in moving the adoption of the minutes in the Town Council, he explaining that it was not intended to keep the same maker for an indefinite period, but only for a short space of time, after which other cements of equal quality would be substituted. The committee wish me to thank you your courtesy in giving them the benefit of your experience in the matter. Yours faithfully. J. LYON WHITTLE. At a subsequent meeting the following further letter was read from the \Varrington Slate Co. Ltd :--Dear you kindly convey to the Chairman and members of the Paving and Sewerage Committee our thanks for the courteous manner in which they have considered our letter of the 7th inst.and the reply which they have been good enough to send us. We are glad to note it is not the intention of the committee to restrict their purchases for an indefinite period to the cement referred to. At the same time may we be per- mitted to say that the difficulty referred to, namely— of obtaining cement of uniform quality from local dealers " would be entire', got rid of by adopting the suggestion made in our 'last letter namely—that the committee should insist upon all cement being up to the standard required by the British Standard Specifica- tion, which is the standard adopted by the Board of Trade in regard to the new Post Office building in Warrington, the cement contract for which we have secured. In regard to this matter may we again repeat, there are any number of firms of high-class reputation each of whom is a manufacturer of cement which will adequately comply with the specification in question. We beg to assure the committee again of our apprecia- tion of the courtesy we have received, and of our desire to do everything which lies in our power to facilitate their work. We beg to remain, yours faith- fully, THE WARRINGTON SLATE CO., LTD. It was resolved, that the Borough Surveyor be autho- rised to temporarily repair Sankey-street from Bank House to Legh-street. A further letter from the Tradesman's Association, in reference to the raising of the curbs in Bridge-street was read, stating that the reply of the Committee was</p>
      </div>
