        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this committee a further letter from I the Volunteer Headquarters as to the use of the land lately belonging to Miss Garven. deceased, was con- sidered : Resolved that the use of the land be granted to the Volunteers for drillinc, purposes, subject to their undertaking to make good all damage done, and to giv- ing up possession of the land on receiving 21 hours notice so to do. " •</p>
        <p>tion of Clerk of Works came before the Committee,who resolved that Mr. F. flaslam be appointed Clerk of Works to superintend the painting of the markets, and that he be paid id. per hour more than the standard rate wages for painters. The work was commenced on the following Monday morning, January 22nd, by twelve unemployed painters, preference being given to married men, which number was increased to thirteen the following week, and maintained until the completion of the work on April 2nd. each man taking a tarn, upwards of GO men in all being employed on the work at the standard rate of wages for painters. The quantities and values of materials used and wages expended in the execution of the work are as follows :</p>
        <p>A letter sanctioning the borrowing of the sum of £7,000 for the purpose of electric lighting was read from _ the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>The Board stated that before sanctioning a further loan for prospective expenditure on cables and services, , the,y will require to be furnished with a statement showing how the loan now sanctioned has been ex- pended, and a map showing the streets in which the mains have been laid. The Board also sanctioned the borrowing of the sum of 11,200 for additionallbacteria beds at Longford Depot on the understanding that no overflow will be con- structed between the collecting tank and the brook. An application on behalf of the Salvation Army for the priceat which the Corporation would be prepared to sell the piece of land in Legh-street, adjoining Messrs. Sutton and Sons' sale rooms was read, and the Deputy Town Clerk was instructed to inform the applicant that the Corporation were not in a position at the present time to dispose of this land. It was resolved that the tender of Mr. W. Wilcock for the outside painting of the following premises according to specification be accepted :—Baths Hotel and premises adjoining, 10s. ; cab stand, Winwick- street, at £5 ; and that the tender of Mr. H. H. Dell for the outside painting of the following premises according to specification be accepted :—Premises occupied by Miss Garner at Market-gate at £1 103. ; premises occu- pied by Messrs. Briggs and Co. and Mr. J. Hall, at Market-gate at 10s. The Borough Treasurer reported that one of the staff of his department (Mr. S. Rathbone) had resigned owing to ill-health ; resolved that the re-arrangement of the staff suggested by the Borough Treasurer be approved, and that he be authorised to engage two junior clerks at a total salary not exceeding 2Os. per week. It was resolved;on the recommendation, of the Street Improvement Committee, that an application be made to the Local Government Board for sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £31,059 its. Gd. for the pur- chase of properties required far street improvements. The minutes were apnroved. HEALTH COMMITTEE. The Medical Officer of Health was authorised to let the land belonging to this committee at Sankey. It was resolved that two sets of bowls be purchased for the use of the consumptive patients at Aikin-street hospital. The minutes were confirmed. STREET IMPROVEMENT COMMITTEE. BIG LOAN WANTED FOR PROPERTY PURCHASES. RESOLUTION WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>s. d. Attendance before Committee Painters' wages, .S:c Various paints 1 ton T:cwt. red and white lead 3l 7 fi 103 gallons oil 48 gallons turpentine 2 dozen paint cans 0 9 3 5 cwt. drjers 2 strainers</p>
        <p>V. dozen brushes 14 pints terebine 6 gallons varnish Knotting and benzoline</p>
        <p>26"8 1 0</p>
        <p>-- 1 From this total of £203 is. a deduction of £1 is made for value of materials left over from the work, leaving a net, total for the carrying out of the painting 467 Is. In addition to the painting, the whole of the painted work was scraped and cleaned over by unem- ployed men at a cost of £66 O. 10d., which, added to the total expended on the painting, £2117 Is., makes a gross total of £333 Es. lod._ The tender of Mr. Richard Tunstall, 191, Padgate- lane, for three shops in the Market Hall, at 27,3. per week, was accepted, subject to Mr. Tuastall entering into all agreement to take the shops on a three months' tenancy,and occupy them for the purpose of his business. The Surveyor reported that to cover the Fair Ground would cost about 4-15,000,and he was instructedto obtain particulars from other towns as to the covering of auction markets there. Alderman ROBERTS moved the adoption of the minutes and pointed out that the men employed in painting the markets received the full rate of wages as paid by Warrington tradesmen. The work had been very well done at is reasonable price. The committee thought that considering the depression of trade at the time it was their duty to find work for as many of the unem- ployed as possible. The committee had thought it would be a desirable thing to cover over the vegetable market but considering the large expenditure it would involve they did not think it wise to entertain the proposal at present. The thanks of the council were due to the Borough Surveyor and clerk of works for their services in connection with the painting of the market. Councillor STOTT said the report was very pleasing to him. The work was done under difficulties and some of</p>
        <p>Messrs. R. Davies and Co. having approached this the men engaged had not been painting for some time Committee with the object of negotiations being and consequently were out of practice. He hoped other c re-opened for acquiring property in Knutsford-roadommittees would follow the example of the Markets C belonging to the executors of the late Mr. Robinson. Committee and employ painters directly. He regretted Resolved that the property be not purchased. however, that two tenders for similar work—one for a The Committee considered the question of the pur- very large sum—had just been accepted. Tlnt council,he chase of a strip of land belonging to the Warrington thought, could find employment for painters all the year Landlnd Property Co., Ltd., at the cornet of Palter- round. I street and Sankey-street, and decided not to purc ass I Alderman BENNETT also thought the experiment the same. Resolved that each member of the Corn- I might be tried in other directions with advantage. It , mittee be supplied with a map of the Borough showing particularly appealed to one in face of the great the building lines which have been prescribed by the I Problem of the unemployed which was so materially Committee. , before us. If the Council could help to solve that The Deputy Town Clerk reported that he had received Problem and get work done satisfactorily and cheaply the draft of an Order proposed to be issued by the, the experiment would be worth the cost. Councillor CR kNTLER objected to the Committee first Local Government Board for the widening of the miss- , 'putting private firms to the trouble of preparing age adjoining the Royal Court Theatre. Mr. W. J. Shaw having approached the Committee ivith the object estimates and then deciding to do the work directly. of their acquiring property belonging to him in Oliver- ' street, resol Councillor BARTON said be was told that if private ved that this property be not purchased, ! contractors had got the work they would have employed The following letter from Mr. Councillor Benson was many of the men whom the committee employed read and considered by the Committee : , directly. Gentlemen,—My suggestions are that providing they I Councillor Maom said the work had been exceed- build a suitable shop for me behind the Church, with a ingly well done for the money but he did not agree frontage in Bridge-street, we might exchange. If they with the suggestion that the precedent should be think it not requisite to opoit the street for 5 or 10 followed in all Corporation work. It was just as reason- years. my present premises, I think, could be let for able for firms like Pearson and' Knowles or Bewsey more than the interest on the cost of building the new forge to adopt the principle and do without masters. shop, and would therefore not increase the present It did not really matter to a man whether he was em- rates. While the Corporation have bad the land that I Ployed by the Corporation or by private concerns so am prepared to treat for, does it not seem a most favour. long as Le received the standard rate. Every man who able time ?—Yours truly, was employed painting the market would be robbing some other man working for a private firm of a job. 11, Sankey-street, Warrington. The Committee did the right thing in this special case, Resolved that M. Councillor Benson he informed however, as there were so many out of employment and that the Committee do not see their way to move in the the work was done honestly and well. matter at present. , The minutes were approved. Resolved that the offer of Mr. J. P. Jones of the sale COST OF SHIP CANAL LITIGATION. by him of property belonging to aim at the corner of Water-street be not entertained. £B,OOO ALREADY PAID. Resolved that the Finance and Estate Committee be At a meeting of the Rivers Committee a letter was recommended to apply to the Local Government Board read from Messrs. A. Wilson and Cowie intimating that for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of they were about to lodge the petition of appeal to the £34,059 us. fid.for the purchase of properties for street House of Lords, and asking the Corporatiao few a improvements, cheque for £lOO, being their proportion „A' the Alderman BENNETT moved the adoption of the minutes necessary cash deposit of .£2OO as security. It was and said the principal item was that recommending the resolved to pay the amount. application foithe borrowing of £34,000 for the purchase The NTAYOR moved the adoption of the minntes. of properties. All the properties had been very carefully Councillor PEMBERTON' i With regard to this appeal, considered by the committee and bought on their</p>
        <p>W. BENSON,</p>
        <p>I was under the impression we had been struck out of the action. The MAYOR, : We have. Councillor PEMBERToN: Then I move that this minute be referred back. The MAYOR said the Council had not been struck out of the!agreement under which they agreed to pay half the costs of the action. They had only been struck out of the action as it went before the tribunal which had to deal with the question. In the judgment there were certain rider clauses that would intertere with the depth of the river. If the judgment were allowed to stand the tradeis using the river would get 18 inches less draught than they were entitled to and the traders therefore thought it time to appeal. The Rivers Com- mittee had joined the traders in saying they ought to get the full depth. That meant an appeal and the matter was in the hands of the lawyers. _ . .</p>
        <p>Year ending Year ending 31st March, 1905. 31st March, 19' Route. Total. Total. Sankey Latchford 4,656 7 01- ... 4,806 12 4 Wilderspool 4,663 6 9,1 ... 5,556 12 Cemotery 3,282 15 4 ... 3,329 12 10. Longford 1,005 3 7 ... 1,100 2 4 Corporation Workmen 39 12 4 ... 33. 10 1</p>
        <p>666.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON thought the position was i unsatisfactory. They had been assured time after time that the action was going to cost them nothing. The MAYOR : We are expecting to get a lot of it back again if you will only be quiet. You don't go to law without paying anything. Councillor PEMBERTON: Those are the terms that the Council agreed to t'.o to law on. Continuing, he I said every member of the committee ought to be I thoroughly acquainted with what had transpired in regard to the payment of the money. As it was, he knew no more about this resolution than the man in the street. Goodness knew what expenditure the appeal would lead them into, in addition to this £lOO. Many mem- bers of the Council would not have agreed to the Corporation joining the law suit if they had not been assured that it was going to cost them nothing. If they had known that the Corporation stood to lose £6,000 they would certainly not have agreed. The MAYOR : If we all knew what was going to happen in the future we would act differently. Councillor PEMBERTON: We ou_ght not to be dragged into one law-suit after another. lam in favour of sup- porting Warrington traders and manufacturers but I object to a certain amount of the ratepayers' money being spent on a few individuals. The MAYOR : The agreement is that we pay one half. Councillor PEMBERTON : We don't get one half of the benefits. . .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: It is not wise to give your case away. This only refcrs to a portion of something carried out years ago and you cannot get out of it. Councillor PEMBERTON : I move that the matter be sent tack for further explanation. Councillor ASHCROFT secon ded.He thought the Council had finished with the action and had done paying. The MAYOR: We are not the acting half in the action but the passive half. We agreed to pay half the expenses and we are bound to pay them. Alderman SMETHURST said he objected to the money being paid. He had constantly objected to the grant- ing of money for this special purpose. His impression was that at the last hearing they got all they wanted. He did not think they ought to go on fighting other people's battles—the whole town having to pay for three or four gentlemen who were quite able to pay themselves. It was time the matter was finally settled. Had the Corporation a definite agreement signed by the traders that they would pay the expenses? The MAYOR : You have an agreement that you will pay half the cost ; that's all. You have to wait for the rest until somebody gets some money back before you can talk about receiving it. Councillor DICKENsON said he was given to under- stand at the committee meeting that the Council were compelled to pay the £lOO but that they would receive a certain sum back. As to when it would come back he could not get any information. (Laughter.) In reply to Councillor Maginn, The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK said the Corporation had paid over the sum of £B,OOO in respect of their share of the costs. The total cost of the action had been about ,000, Of course, the action had been won and those costs would be taxed. The costs returned after taxation would be divided equally between the Corporation and the traders. Councillor MAGINN : Does the £lOO come out of the £B.OOO or is it additional ? The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK: It is additional. Councillor MAGINN :How many more '£ lOO's are still to be paid by the Corporation ? The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK : I cannot say that, not being a prophet, but I should think the taxed costs we get back will pay for it. Mr. Hallaway further stated that the Corporation had been struck out of the action and the solicitors were carrying on the appeal on behalf 1 of the traders. Councillor iVrAcaNs Where has the £16,000 gone? Mr. HALLAWAY : In counsel's fees, witnesses, &amp;c. Councillor MAom said it was a great shame that I Warrington should have fallen out with the Canal ICompany. They had made the company their enemies land had poured money into the hands of the lawyers, like water through a seive, without benefit to the Cor- poration. He did not suppose anyone could tell what the limit of their expense would be. The MAYOR: —lt is limited when you have finished. The agreement that is being carried out was made before I was chairman of the Rivers Committee. Councillor MA G INN : I hope this Council will never leave a heritage for coming Councils like we have had to handle from others. The MAYOR : If I had been chairman of the Rivers Committee years ago I would have altered it. Councillor BURTON said the Corporation supported the Canal Company when they applied for the Act of Parliament, but the Act when passed was unfair to the traders who depended on the river for the success of their works. It therefore became necessary for the Corporation to stand up in defence of the traders. In all fairness to the traders it was incumbent on the Council to see that justice was done. The 11/Lt ;on. said this payment of £lOO would probably be the last and it was no use "kicking up a row " now, after spending £B,OOO. When they had won the action and something was introduced to neutralize the effect, it would be foolishness to swop horses when crossing the stream. The Canal Company asked that the Corporation should pay half of their costs as well as half of the traders, but that was struck out. The Corporation and the traders had stood together and the Corporation had been saved those costs asked for by the Canal Company. The traders in trying to get damages had proved the Corporation's case with regard to the depth of the river. It would be foolish to squabble about £lOO when the goal was in sight. They might have to pay one or two hundred pounds, but be did not think they could have to pay many. The amendment was defeated, only the proposer and the seconder voting for it, and the minutes were confirmed. •</p>
        <p>- -- 17,893 11 7</p>
