      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee resolved that the tender of Messrs H. Simmais and Co., of London, for the supply of a 60 gallon combined chemical fire extinctertr and escape (60 feet, long) according W specification at £299 be accepted, subject to satisfactory trials being given in accordance with the Company's guarantee and to such other trials as may be required by the committee. The Borough Surveyor was instructed to have a connection fixed in the Superintendent's house to enable the fire. bell to be rung more quiclrly in case of fires occurring in the night time. The minutes were agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>TRAMWAYS. RESULT OF THE YEAR'S WORKING.</head>
        <p>THE TRAMWAYS. RESULT OF THE YEAR'S WORKING.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee the deputy Town Clerk reported that the expenses for obtaining an extension of time for the purchase of land for and the construction of Light Rail- way No. 2 would probably be about £l5O. Resolved that the resolution of the 21th April, instructing such application to be made, be rescinded. On the recom- mendation of the Sub-Committee appointed to consider tenders for clothing, the following tenders were accepted according to specification and schedule of , prices :—Messrs. Brunt and Co., for clothing, Mr. J. Banks, for caps. The tender of Mr. J. Johnson for the repair and up keep of the tramway track according to specification and schedule of prices for six months was accepted, subject to approved security being given in the sum of £5O. The Borough Treasurer submitted the annual statement of account from the Electricity Department, which showed a net profit of ,£2,793. It was resolved, that the system of workmen's return tickets be extended to the Cemetery route. A deputation from the Parish Council of Stockton Heath waited upon the Committee and made the follow- ing requests :—(1) That a penny fare should be instituted from Stockton Heath to Ryland's-street • (2) That a Id. stage should be instituted from Fletcher- stieet to Stafford-road. The Committee, after giving the fullest consideration to khe matter, decided that it was not practicable to malte any changes at the present time. A petition was received from a number of workmen employed at the Smkey Wire Mills, asking that the times during which workmen's tickets were available should be extended. Resolved, that no action be taken in the matter at present. In moving the adoption of the minutes Alderman Borough Treasurer apgruerseerntiend rtehgeardfoltloowtihneg yreeapro,artwofrrkoimogthtoe the tramways:—l beg to report that for the year ending the 31st March last, the gross profit on the tramways undertaking amounted to £6,205 16s. sd. as against 26,246 15s. 2d. for the corresponding period last year. The sinking fund and interest charges have been met out of thisleaving a pet profit of £472 17s.4d.as against £450 Ss. 6d. fhis sum is sufficient to wipe out the debit balance of £241 19s. id. with which we started the year, and leaves a balance in hand of £230 l6s. 3d. The following statement shows the working expenses per car mile for the year, as against those for the year ending 31st March, 1905:</p>
        <p>1905. 1900. Traffic expenses per car mile 2.72 ... 2.657 General expenses per car mile Repairs ,&amp; Maintenance do Power expenses do</p>
        <p>' 6308 6751 This increased cost of working has been caused by the heavier maintenance charges, which have gone up from 21,281 to £2,001. The passenger receipts have increased, the total being £17,896,0r 10165 per car mile, as against £16,599, or 9•876 d. per car mile. The following com- parative statement gives the income upon the different routes for the years ending 31st March, 1905, and 31st March, 1900 respectively :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>16,599 5 3</head>
        <p>16,599 5 3</p>
        <p>The Capital Expenditure under the different Sanctions to 31st. March, 1906, was as follows : Amount Sanctioned. Exp're. Unexpended First Sanction Second ~ Lt. Rlys. Order</p>
        <p>-- 128,030 101,065 8 11 26,981 11 1</p>
        <p>With regard to the electricity undertaking Mr. Fair- hurst reported For the year ending the 31st March last, the gross profit on the electricity undertaking amounted to £7,968 12s. 10d., as against £6,505 ss. id., for the year ending 31st March, 1905. The Sinking Fund and interest charges, amounting to £5,205 13s. 3d. have been paid out of this, leaving a profit of £2,762 19s. id., as against a profit of £1,513 12s. 9d. for the previous year. Not- withstanding the decreased charge for current, the income has increased by £2,177, shown under the follow- ing headings, viz : Ordinary Consumers Current for Trams 390 Meter Rents ittings Miscellaneous</p>
        <p>£2.177 Against this, the total expenditure has only increased by £958, generation having increased by £520, distribu- tion £66' public lamps £2B, management £lOO, and Sinking Fund and Interest charges £244. The net profit has been transferred to the Reserve Fund, which now amounts to £4,135. The capital expended during the year was £3,096, while the loans outstanding at 31st March last amounted to £77,155. The Sinking Fund h-as £1,187 Is. 7d. standing to its credit, and the debt extinguished from the commencement of the under- taking to date, amounts to £6,416. Mr. Fair- hurst had also prepared a statement showing that the:estimated expenditure in the electricity depart- ment for the year 1906-7 was £7,037 18s. id. as against £5,961 135. 43. for last year. It was pointed out that the rates to be paid by the department were estimated to increase by £5OO. Councillor PEAIBERTON seconded. While Alderman Smethurst looked at things through rosy spectacles he did not take exactly the same view. They spent on the two undertakings £200.000 and a miserable £3,000 profit was abominable. It did not justify the expenditure and served to show how municipalitieslrushed into things. No doubt the committee would have to meet difficulties in the future, and as the life of a tramway and electrical works was not very long they could not expect to recoup themselves out of the profits after paying capital and interest. The profit was not at all adequate and ' was not what it ought to be considering the enormous Icapital expenditure. They all knew their electricity works were too big and to make them pay they must work them to their full capacity. While to make some profit was satisfactory he would not be content until the undertakings made an adequate return. Alderman BENNETT congratulated Alderman Smethurst on the position which he had been able to announce. He thought thefigures vindicated the attitude of those who agreed with municipal trading judiciously and properly conducted. The profit instead of being £3,000 was really £15,000. Opponents of municipal trading always forgot sinking fund and interest and the Corporation was paying back the capital all the time. If they ran things on similar lines to a private firm and allowed a sum for deprecia- tion they would be able to show avery decent dividend. He quite agreed that the profit was not as much as it ought to be, but they must work the undertakings for all they were worth and hope for better things in the future. Councillor BURTON asked why the petition from the employees of Sankey Wire Mills had been refused. Was it owing to the abuse of the tickets or to the poverty of the committee? Councillor l'vlasi INN said he could not allow the minutes to pass without congratulating Alderman Smethurst. He was sorry he could not agree with Alderman Bennett with regard to municipal trading. There were certain things which a Corporation could take in hand and work well but there were other things which they entered into with blinded eyes and he was afraid that was the way they had entered upon the tram- ways. With regard to the deputation from Stockton Heath in respect to reduced fares to the village, he appealed to those gentlemen that if they were in the position of the Corporation they would act as the Corporation had acted. They spent £lO,OOO on the quarter of a mile of track through the village and even with the extra half-penny the traffic would not bring that money back. One argument was that there were 300 workmen in Stockton Heath who were employed in Warrington but why should the Corpora- tion carry them cheaply to earn their money and then carry them outside the town to spend it? Another argument was that with cheaper fares people would come from Stockton Heath to Warrington to spend their money. He was sorry to have to expose that to the Stockton Heath tradesmen. With respect to the petition, those men were working overtime and being paid time and a quarter and if they were not prepared to pay their moiety towards an undertaking, the profits of which went towards the rates, they ought to be ashamed of themselves. If they were not ashamed of sending in the petition he was not ashamed of being one who had refused to grant it. Alderman SMETHIIRST said he did not agree with Councillor Pemberton that the electricity works were too large. They could not have done with one yard less space than they had. He contended that workmen were being most handsomely treated. They found that the old system of workmen's fares was working with dis- advantage to the cars. On fine days the men would walk and when it was wet they would ride for a half- penny and crowd out of the seats those who paid the full fare. The minutes were approved. GENERAL PURPOSES CONIMITTEE. WARRINGTON BRIDGE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Tt Was resolved, that the following minutes of the Bridges Sub-committee be approved and adopted : That Mr. J. J. Webster's account of £232 Us. 3d. for his professional services in connection with the pre- paration of the plans, &amp;c., of Warrington Bridge. be I passed to the GeneralVurposes Committee for payment; that the General Purposes Committee be recommended to appoint the chairman, Alderman Evans and Coun- cillors Burton, Duncan and Pemberton, as a sub-com- mittee to report on the method of constructing bridges on the Ferro-Concrete princfple, with power to inspect one or more bridges already constructed; that Mr. H. Rtirclough be paid the sum of £lO Ws. in settlement of his claim for loss incurred through the Corporation not proceeding with his contract. Councillor EVANS asked if there was any agreement between the London and. North Western Railway Com- pany and the Council with regard to Wilderspool cross- ings. A considerable number of accidents had happehed to horses through the crossings and he would like to know whether there was any agreement as to liability. The DEPUTY TOWN alum explained that there was no agreement but the Ccirpotation did not accept any liability. In fact they were not liable. Councillor EvANs said that in the event of a horse being injured ho wanted to know who was liable 9,nd who would be sued for damages. Mr. FIALLAWAY : The Corporation is not liable. The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR WILSON AND WORKMEN'S TRAM FARES.</p>
        <p>Councillor WrLsox had given notice that he would move" That it be optional with passengers using the Workmen's Cars, either morni,ng or evening, to take a single or return ticket as they luny desire." The MAYOR pointed out that at present the workmen could take a single ticket. Councillor Wtr,sos : I withdraw the motion if they can take a halfpenny ticket to Warrington— The MAYOR: The motion says nothing about half- penny tickets, and you cannot amend it. Councillor WILSON: That is only a quibble. The MAYOR : Ask the Chairman of the Committee to bring it up at the next meeting. The motion was accordingly withdrawn. ___ CORPORATION PAINTING.</p>
        <p>COLlneillOr—S—TOTTs';'oVei'Tiu;rit: be an instruction to all Committees of the Council and the Education Committee to arrange as far as possible for the painting and decorating of public buildings and schools to be done during the winter months.r The MAYOR, on behalf of the Education Committee, said that Committee would, as far as was possible, fall in with Councillor Stott's wishes. Councillor STOTT said ho hoped the Committee would bear the motion in mind. There was very little work for painters in the winter time, and if the Corporation could assist them by having their work done during that four or five months, they would be helping a deserving class of men. He hoped the, motion would not be a pious expression of opinion, but that it would be acted upon. Councillor MAGINN seconded. He criticisel the painters for improvidence during the summer months, but agreed that it would be a great pity if the Cor- poration could not defer work until the winter in order to assist those men. The motion was unanimously carried. TETE CONVICTION OF MR. A. PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallway produced a copy of the conviction of Mr. A. Peacock which he had laid before the Council. Councillor STOTT asked who paid the costs of the witnesses ? Mr. HALLAwAY : The Corporation. The defence pay their own costs. Councillor STOTT : Who paid Alderman Francomb ? Mr. HALLAWAY : We did not pay him. This concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE. GLORIOUS WEATHER FOR BANK HOLIDAY. More delightful weather than that which favoured the Whitsuntide holiday could hardly have been desired. Following on the unsettled conditions of last week, it was doubly appreciated and holiday makers revelled in bright sunshine at all the favourite haunts by the sea and in the country. Saturday was promising, although the high wind interfered with the enjoyment of the many cyclists who were awheel in the Lancashire and Cheshire lanes. On Sunday the sun again shone forth radiantly and it was indeed pleasant to be out of doors. At the Anglican and Roman Catholic Churches the day as usual was observed as one of the greatest festivals of the year and the numerous services were well attended. From an early hour on Monday morning the railway stations presented ,an animated scene, some thousands of There is every probability of a contest for the repro- people being anxious to make their way to ..entation of Orford Ward on the Council, rendered "fresh fields and pastures new." Splendid facilities vacant by the conviction of Mr. A. Peacock. Mr. Elijah were provided by the railway companies and crowded Cunnington has consented to stand in the interests of trains left for all parts. The following are some of the Non-traders and Mr. William Rogers, landlord of the Feathers iHotel, has also been nominated. This the principal bookings: —Bank Quay -Station : Bit- afternoon (Friday) s the last day for withdrawal and mingham, Wolverhampton and Shrewsbury, 200; in the event of both gentlemen seeking the suffrages Frodsham, 600 ; Chester and North Wales, 600 ; and of the burgesses the election will take place next Blackpool, 200. Central Station : Liverpool, 1,000 ; Friday. Two nomination papers have been handed in New Brighton, 550; Manchester, 600; Southport, 650; on behalf of Mr. Cunnington. In the first he is pro- boat bookings for North Wales, 150 ; and Douglas, posed by Thomas Hill, seconded by John Thomas Harri- -100. So fickle has our climate been of late, that the son,and supported by Thomas Mullins, Thomas Grundy, Richard G. W. Lake, Thomas Shaw. Jossph Bunch, early trippers went away carrying overcoats and Frederick Greaves, Joseph Bullock and Zacharias Kent. umbrellas in preparation for any emergency. In the second paper he is proposed by Thomas Hard- They were not needed, for soon after nine man, seconded by Robert Bloomfield and supported by o'clock the clouds rolled away and throughout Edward Hughes, Thomas Taylor, J. D. Drinkwater, jun., the day there was a blue sky and brilliant James Harrison, Richard Brown, Charles Hamnond, sunshine. Cyclists enjoyed themselves to their heart's John Brown and Henry Marshall. content. Picturesque Cheshire was looking at its William Rogers is proposed by John Grounds, best and large numbers made the journey through seconded by William James Irvine, and supported by Charles L. Rogers, Charles Hall, Joseph Gerrard, John the lovely surroundings of Delamere Forest to demanand,Lammeeas gerwouerls, Peter Wells, James Oulton Park where the military tournament was 1 Edward Loan': attended with great success. At home the sports pro- moted by Messrs. Crosfield's Recreation Club attracted Mr. William Rogers withdrew. from the contest a large concourse and keen interest -was taken in the I to-day at 2 o'clock. various events. The Whit-Monday of 1905 will certainly be remembered as one of the most successful Bank Holidays on record.</p>
        <p>' . , • • 4'4", • ';</p>
