      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>CROSFIELD S SPORTS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD S SPORTS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The only attraction in Warrington on Whit-Monday was the annual athletic meeting in connection with the Recreation Club of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Limited, and the event was extensively patronized. The weather was gloriously fine and some three thousand spectators assembled on the Warrington football ground where an excellent programme was provided. The whole of the races were confined to Messrs. Cros- field's employees, and the promoters would perhaps be well advised if in future they introduced one or two open events as this would have a tendency to increase the interest in the proceedings. Valuable prizes were offered and attracted a large number of entries, though unfortunately in some of the events, only a few com- petitors actually turned out. Take for instance the two miles bicycle handicap. Thirteen riders entered their names and five turned out. Two heats were run and the first, second, and third in each were entitled to run in the final. In heat one three rode and qualified for the final after a considerable amount of crawling. Obviously it would have been much better had the race been ' decided in one heat. Generally speaking, however, the sports had been excellently arranged, and judicious handicapping resulted in close finishes. One of the finest races ot the afternoon was the final for the 100 yards. A. Whittaker and H. Hartley were in winners, both having done the fastest time n the heats. All were off the mark well, and Whittaker at once got to the front, until a few yards from the tape when Hartley and Lightfoot forged ahead and breasted tape first, Hartley leading by a foot. At the conclusion of the sports the prizes were presented by Miss Markel. Appended are the results of the various events :- 220 Yards Flat Race (youths under 17 years of age).— Heat winners : C. Billington, 25 : J. Nelding, is ; H. Shakeshaft, 15 ;J. H. Golding, 15 ;H. Griffiths, ; and H. J. Smith, 10. Final : J. H. Goldihg, 1;H. J. Smith, 2 ; H. Griffiths, 3. 1.00 Yards Flat Race.—Heat winners: S. Shipley, 5 ; R. Dunbobbin,2 ;J. A. Watson, 5 ; A. Whittaker, 6 • H. Hartley, 5; E. Lightfoot, 8i; W. P. Heywood, 7 : S. Lightf not 10. Final ;H. Bartley, 1 ; E. Lightfoot, 2 A. Whittaker, 3. Time, 10 2-5 secs. ; One Mile Bicycle.—Heat winners : J. Ray, 20 ; J. Orinson, 90 ; J. Bennett, 135; J. Bruhl, 65 ' • C. Dag n 11 S;W. J. Brown, GO. Final : James Ray, 1 •J. li 2; J. Ormson, 3. Won by three lengths;wheel. Tug of War.—ln the preliminary rounds Cooperage beat Shipping No. 1, and Shipping No. 2, beat Glycerine The final was hotly contested and after a good pull Cooperage won. 300 Yards Obstacle,—Heat winners, S. Sunderland,2o; J.C. Holden, 14 ; H. Brimley, 17; C. INlillson, 28 ; E. Gibs9n, 10 ; A. Roberts, 29; W. A. Kinsey, 22 : A. Bennett, 20 ;A. Roach, 25. Final : E. Gibson 1; A. Roberts, 2 ; J. C. Holden, 3. 80 Yards Veterans' Final Race.—Heat winners, P. Johnson,2 ; H. Skelland, S ; R. Nolland, 1; H. Skeiland, 2; P. Johnson 3. 100 Yards Uniform Race.—Heat Winners ; John Newnes, scratch ; A. Herschel!, 8 ; E. Lightfoot, 4 : H. Bromley, ; Final : Newnes 1; E. Lightfoot, 1 ' • H. Bromley. 60 yards Egg and Spoon Race (girls).—Heat winners : B: Churn, J. Williams, E. Sharpies, C. Widen, E. Platt, and H. Dodd. Final : E. Sharpies, 1 ; J. Williams, 2 ; B. Churn, 3. 100 Yards (men over 36)—Heat Winners : T. Tindall I scratch ; R. Nolland, 12 ; D. Jones, 6' • J. Barlow, I scratch. Final : T. Tindall, 1 ; R. Nolland. 2 ; D. Jones, 3. 80 Yards (Girls)—Heat Winners : B. Churn H. Gleave, L. Hewitt, R. M. Kendrick, E. Long, A. Warburton. Final : B. Churn, 1 ; A. Warburton, 2; L. Hewitt, 3. 440 Yards—Heat Winners : R. Dunbobbin, 4 ; James Long, 16; W. T. Heywood, 20 C. Libbetter, 25 ; E. Lightfoot, 23 ; F'. Tomlinson, 12 ; S. Shipley, 20 ; E. W. Warren, 12; A. Whittaker,2s. Final : R. Dunbobbin, 1; S. Shipley, 2 ; J. Long, 3. Half-Mile Flat.—James Hands (45), 1 ; E. Lightfoot (8), 2 ; James Long (6), 3. One Mile Bicycle.—J. Bruhl (140), 1 ; W. J. Brown (140), 2 ; J. °Nilson (180), 3. - One Mile Flat Race.-1, W. Welsby (140) ; 2, Joseph Riley (scratch) ; 3, T. Devilin (30). One Mlle Walking Handicap.-1, J. Bibby (120) ; 2, R. Nolland (190) ; 3, J. Kendrick (190). A word of praise is due to the officers for their excel- lent arrangements. They were : Judges, H. Newall, F. J. Lord, E. Pownall, T. Davies, H. Bell, T. Rigby, J. Dutton, and J. Rose • Marksmen, J. A. Watson, E. W. Warren, W. Bailey, G. Podmore, W. Brobbin, J. Potts, T. Knowles, J. Bruhl, J. Newnes, and J. Riley ; Clerk of course, J. Gallon ; Starter, J. Fair ; Hon. Treasurer, H. Clayton, Hon. Secretaries, George Gillin and E. M. Wright.</p>
