        <p>• ~,_ CURIOUS CRIMINAL CHUIGE. Heyes, collier, agel fifty-five years, who had the last four or five months lodged with some s°Ple named Lea, who keep a small shop at 1. Appleton- Street, was, at St. ilelens ' Police Court, on Monday, a ent for trial to the Assizes, charged with a grave 'l•3l3sault on a woman. The offence was alleged to have 1,550 commited during Friday night, when the woman's ;.Ausband was out at a neighbour's ph:dui:cards. Reyes ,s alleged to have personated the woman's husband, and When she discoverad the mistake the woman raised the neighbourhood, and Police Constable Merrill arrested " HeYes, who treated the matter very lightly, and said, I want a holiday, and I shall get only about three or four months for this." He boasted about the !natter, an at the time of his arrest showed very great Indifference. Hayes did not deny the evidence, and '‘vas committed for trial.</p>
        <p>G.W.R.</p>
        <p>• moan oecame sobs. She floundered lila rt,gteat, ship into the arms ot the women-4..w. Si ilietoe asking Ben, as if on behalf of his wile. "But tha' feet, lad ? He also was faint now, and muttered: "They're : still th' clogs, Ann; still i' th' clogs—" and they took him to rest on the oblong kitchen table lista the arrival of the doetor. ittat night Jim Holden received by special messenger the long petition form. There was ki (laity Meeting of all the men in Doddy's Noois. rhay decided to get to work at once. Well, the reight hand to head th' list is Bel) .I.arsenshaw's," said Jim. 'True, lad, true. nice tale' it to-neight—afeas he sienna see th' mornin'. Aw'd go wi' theft, on'y my wife's timer; her says he's sinkin'—ar.' this can manage Betty better nor any on us." Jim rolled the petition compactly, and thrust it up his sleeve like a white ramrod. He found Ben much worse, moaning his agonies or. the table which had been turned into a bed, the women looking on, amid the gloonrof a single candle. "How ista now, Ben? 7 "Who's theer ? " asked Ben, too absorbed in his tortures to open his eyes. "Jim—Jim Howden." "Well, hes it cbm'd ? " "Aye, Ben; an' we want thy hand to lead off." "Tha'll not hey his hands in it as well !" called Betty. "He's awriddy, led off teo much wi' his feet for thy Joe Clayton! "Aye," said the women. "Now tuck yon gallus paper up thy sleeve , agen." cried Betty, an' pack out o' this!" "Dunna wear me down, Betty'!" appealed Ben. "Thr. may hey bin reight afore, but Jim an' me's reight now. Wheer s th' pen, lad? " "'Mall not!" cried Betty. 'Dia canna know what tha'rt dealin' wi'.. He isna in his healthy senses--an' hesna' bin this day or two!" "Lift me up, Jim, an' gi'e us th' pen." "Now tha'lt not put thy hand to yon scroll I " "Tha'rt killing me, ooman, kiln' me! Now, just hark ye a bit., Betty, owd girl. Joe Clayton wor my-0 Lord A-meightv Ileav'n I" he cried with pitin—"aw seem to be svalkin' hell wi' what used to be my feet." (Betty turned away, crying.) "Gi 'e us a sup o' wavier, some o' ye." "Ov coorse we will ! " said Ann Sillictoe, as nother woman silently ran for water. "Now, ip thee, lad." 'Aye. Jim, how'd on to this big yed o' mine a bit. Tip up th' cup, wench, an' let's get at it. My sakes! but freesia winter wayter's nice, Ann, when dm tongue's a little blazes all to iteen !" and his sunken eyes eagerly sought for Betty among the grouped women. He saw her bent broad back shaking over the hand-rail post at the [cot of the stairs. "0 Betty!" he cried, "dunna, dunna ! Jotl Dia ton wor my crony at school; wheer aw were went;Se what he did aw did • we rabbitei asigither , we bird-nested ; we shared miekies ; wi nrectirearted togither—" "An' see what he did wi' hie," answered Betty, crying. "Kick'd her to her grave—" "An' hissen under tit gallus," replied Ben; "an' when aw wor dead-done tinder th' aifter- damp th' sixty-one flrin'—who carried me in Lis arms to th' air o life? " "Aye, indeed, Betty," argued the women. "Joe Clayton did do that." "Hey, ate durum want his life, women. All tint, is that livin or dead, he'll leave Ben in' me alone." "Gi'e tie th' pen, Jim I" But Betty, only partly resigned, and still fearing the innermost fate of the event, opened the front door, and half-closed it upon herself. There she gazed at the brilliant winter stars, trying to read aim like the vast alphabet of Fate momentarily written and erased on the great blackboard of the unisarse. What did•a falling star mean? What did a trembling, twinkling, low on 3? What did a cloud wiping out thiee stars at a time mean? Ben was sitting up, pale, and in agonizing pain. "It wor JoftsClayton hisser., more nor th' school- 'nester," he said, carefully fixing the pen between his quivering 'firgers, "as first shoved me B's an' e's all' n's. Phew ! lift th' weight o' th' quilt o 5 down theer, Ann ! An' it's queer fer me to corn' to write it in a way aw nivver writ it afore, fee Shari; slowly he wrote "B "—so slowly that it looked as if lie were doing it on the sly. Slowly he completed "Ben," suddenly dropped the pen, cried aloud With torture as he pitifully looked down the bed where his feet would have been if he bad had them on. Some preternatural pangs seemed to travel up his form and quake his hand. "Theft better not trouble, Ben; appealed a wman. "Put it i' th' fingers agen, Jim!" laboriously most laboriouly wrote "Birk," but had to Bt,p, again looking down his tortured limbs. He shortly growled: " Fix th' stick i' my fist agrn ! " He tried. But could not. His hand seemed locked. He sank back murmuring. "What's silin' me!" he moaned, mystified. - " Do it for him, Jim," suggested Ann Sillietoe. "Dom me! " cried Ben, "aw'll do it if aw hey to sten' up on my stumps!" and he made a vigorous attempt to rise. But they held him down. "Try agen, lad," appealed Jim. He tried, lie could only add two letters. He fell back with a deadly pallor. A woman's shriek called for Betty. But Betty, without hearing it, opened the door, saying: "Sithee ! E'en th' moon's like a clog, an' red--" "Huslit ! Thee luk round at Ben !" "0 Ben—Denial! lad! Aw shotild hey stood agen it! Aw wor reight at fitic—" "Husht ! husht ! Gi'e him veace." uOn'y one thing 'II save him! " she cried,snatch- jog the petition from Jim Holden's hand, "an' ' that's—that " The petition was criished, and thrust down into , the fire. Jim Holden forgot himself in an oath. Betty, fiercely held the paper down until the smoke became flame, gazing at Ben as if watch- ing the result of a magic spell that would revive him •' but the great spell which held them all bound in its thrall was the magic spell o' 'Deaths ' Ll'as Elm] .410. A WORLD-WIDE ORGANIZATION. Shakespeare spoke of puttinga girdle round about the arth in forty minutes. That feat has been accomplished n sober fact, if not in forty minutes at any rate , in the comparatively short space of twenty years, by the Columbia Phonograph Company, General, the largest talking machine manufacturers in the world. During that time they have opened up a chain of establishments which literally girdle the earth. In all the chief cities throughout the United Statea, in Australia, in the capital of practically every state in Europe and of each constituent country of Great Britain, this enterprising company have large and important branches. And they Are now conducting a beneficent invasion of the English provinces. The month of May has seen the opening of a new branch in hl4nchester. The address is 54, Market-street, and the handsome . building serves to demonstrate the standing of the ' Columbia Company. The denizens of the North and Midlands will appreciate the special facilities which this new branch offers them, while everyone will recognize in the magnitude and continued growth of the concern, a guarantee of the excellence of Columbia Graphophones and Records: In addition to their own branches the Columbia Company have dealers every- where. A postcard to the European Headquarters, ns, 1 Great Eastern-street, London, E.C. for "Price Book 20" will bring particulars of all the new models in Cylinder and Disc Columbia Graphophones. A MOTORIST AND HIS LICENCE. At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday last,- Herbert Sankey was summoned at the instance of P.C. Powell I for driving a motor cycle without being licensed to do so. He pleaded ignoionee of the fact that his licence to drive had to be renewed ; he renewed his motor licence, and carried the receipt for his driving licence about with him, thinking he had a permanent permit for driving.—Fined 10s. and costs.</p>
