        <p>The Popularity of the Trailer will probably never be of a very extensive or general character, and the trailer itself is probably a more suitable adjunct a the motor 'cycle than of the push bicycle. At the same time, there can be no denying that many people find the trailer a highly useful vehicle (if locomotion, even when used with the humble pedal bicycle. A fiend of the writer's liNdng in a small Sussex town travels into Eastbourne regularly once a week• with his wife in a trailer, visits the theatre, and reaches home again well before midnight, which would be impossible but for the trailer. A Motor-Assisted Cycle. Mr. R. J. Urquhart, of Manchester, is the inventor of a motor•assisted bicycle, the feature of which consists of an improved system of variable speed- driving gear, in conjunction with pedal assistance. This gear is contained in the back hub of a machine driven by an engine of about h.p., and the whole machine complete comes out at about 55 lb. in weight, the engine and carburetter weigh- ing 14 lb. The engine can be disconnected or connected with the road wheel at the pleasure of the rider. A peculiarity is that, while the number of pedal revolutions modifies the gear ratio between the engine and the road wheel, the engine cannot affect the pedals—that is, it cannot drive the rider's pedals round against his will. The gear can be varied to suit any hill by slow continuous pedalling, according to the speed desired, while on the level the h.p. engine is so geared as to take the machine at ten or twelve miles an hour on a level road without pedal assistance.</p>
